\ide UTF-8
\h 2 Chronicles
\toc1 The Second Book of the Chronicles
\toc2 Second Chronicles
\toc3 2Ch
\mt1 2 Chronicles
\c 1
\p
\v 1 Solomon son of David became firmly in control of his kingdom. Yahweh his God helped him and made him a very great king.
\p
\v 2 Solomon called together many Israelite leaders, including the officers who commanded groups of a thousand soldiers and groups of a hundred soldiers. He also called together the judges and other leaders in Israel who governed clans.
\v 3 Solomon and all those leaders went together to the hilltop shrine at Gibeon. They went there because God’s sacred tent was there. Moses, Yahweh’s servant, had made that tent while the Israelites were traveling through the desolate area between Egypt and Canaan.
\v 4 Now the sacred chest was no longer there because David and the Israelite leaders had brought it from the city of Kiriath Jearim to Jerusalem. David had prepared a place for it by setting up a tent for it.
\v 5 However, the bronze altar that Bezalel had made was still at Gibeon. Bezalel was the son of Uri and the grandson of Hur. That altar was in front of the sacred tent. So Solomon and the Israelite leaders prayed to Yahweh there.
\v 6 So Solomon went to the bronze altar in front of the sacred tent, where Yahweh was present. He offered a thousand animals as sacrifices that the priests burned completely on the altar.
\p
\v 7 That night, God appeared to Solomon {in a dream}. He told him, “Tell me what you want, and I will give it to you.”
\p
\v 8 Solomon replied to God, “You always loved my father David very faithfully, and now you have made me the next king of Israel.
\v 9 Yahweh my God, you have made me the king of a nation that has very many people, just as there is very much dust on the earth. So please do what you promised to my father David {and make me a successful king}.
\v 10 Please enable me to be wise and to know what I should do. Then I will be able to rule these people well. Otherwise, I would not be able to rule this great nation of yours.”
\p
\v 11 God replied to Solomon, “You did not ask to become very rich or famous. You did not ask that would you be able to kill your enemies. You also did not ask to live for a long time. Instead, you asked me to make you wise and help you know what to do. Then you would be able to decide fairly the cases of the people whose king I have made you. Because that is what you wanted,
\v 12 I will make you wise and help you know what to do. But I will also make you very rich and famous, more than any king was before you or will be after you.”
\v 13 Then Solomon and the leaders who were with him left the sacred tent that was at the hilltop shrine in Gibeon. They returned to Jerusalem, and from there Solomon ruled the Israelite people.
\p
\v 14 Solomon acquired a great force of chariots and riders. In his army, he had 1,400 chariots and 12,000 men who rode on horses. Solomon had built special cities in which to keep chariots, and he kept many of his chariots in them. But he also kept some chariots with him in Jerusalem.
\v 15 During the years that Solomon was king, silver and gold became very common in Jerusalem, just as stones are common. Cedar wood became very plentiful, just as the wood is plentiful that comes from the sycamore trees that grow in the foothills of Judah.
\v 16 Solomon imported his horses from Egypt and Kue. His purchasing agents would go to Kue and buy horses there at the current price.
\v 17 In Egypt, his agents bought both horses and chariots. They paid about seven kilograms of silver for each chariot and about two kilograms of silver for each horse. Agents of Solomon would then resell many of the horses and chariots to Hittite and Aramean kings.
\c 2
\p
\v 1 Solomon decided to build a temple to honor Yahweh and a royal palace.
\v 2 He required Israelite men to become his workers. 70,000 of them would carry heavy loads. 80,000 of them would cut stones from quarries in the hills. 3,600 other men would supervise them.
\p
\v 3 Solomon sent this message to King Hiram of the city of Tyre: “When my father David was building a palace to live in, you sent him cedar logs. Please do the same for me.
\pi1
\v 4 I ask this because I am going to build a temple to honor Yahweh my God. It will be a special place where we will worship him. We will burn fragrant spices as incense to him. Our priests will put fresh bread in rows on a special table every week. They will burn sacrifices completely on an altar every morning and evening. They will also do that on Sabbath days, on New Moon festivals, and at each of the feasts that we celebrate each year to honor Yahweh our God. We Israelites must always do these things.
\v 5 The temple that I am going to build must be a very great temple, because our God is greater than all other gods.
\v 6 (But no one is really able to build a place for God to live in, since the whole sky and the spiritual realm beyond the sky are not big enough for him. I myself do not deserve to build a temple for him except as a place to offer sacrifices to him.)
\v 7 So please send me someone who can make things expertly from gold, silver, bronze, and iron. He should also be able to make things expertly from purple, red, and blue cloth. He should also be able to carve designs in wood very skillfully. I want him to work here in Jerusalem and in other places in Judah with the skilled craftsmen whom my father David trained.
\pi1
\v 8 I know that your workers are able very skillfully to cut down the great trees that grow in Lebanon. So please also send me cedar logs, cypress logs, and algum logs from there. My workers will work together with your workers.
\v 9 In that way, they will be able to provide me with much lumber. We will need much because the temple that I am going to build will be large and beautiful.
\v 10 I will agree to pay your workers, the men who cut the logs, 4,400 kiloliters of ground wheat, 4,400 kiloliters of barley, 440 kiloliters of wine, and 440 kiloliters of olive oil.”
\p
\v 11 King Hiram of Tyre responded by sending a letter to Solomon. He wrote, “Yahweh has made you the king of his people because he loves them.
\v 12 He also wrote, “I thank Yahweh, the God of the Israelites, who created the sky and the land, for giving King David such a wise and intelligent son! He will build a temple for Yahweh and a royal palace.
\v 13 I agree to send you Huram Abi, one of my skilled craftsmen.
\v 14 His mother was {an Israelite} from the tribe of Dan, and his father was from here in Tyre. He is able to make things expertly from gold, silver, bronze, iron, stone, and wood. He is also able to make things expertly from purple, blue, linen, and red cloth. He is able to carve various designs in wood very skillfully. He can make things using any design that you give to him. He will work with your craftsmen and with the craftsmen who worked for your father, King David.
\pi1
\v 15 Now please send us the wheat, barley, olive oil, and wine that you promised to send to us.
\v 16 My workers will cut from the mountains in Lebanon all the logs that you need. They will bring the logs to you by tying them together to form rafts and floating them by sea to the city of Joppa. From there, your workers can bring them to Jerusalem.”
\p
\v 17 Solomon had his servants count all the men from other countries who were living in Israel, just as his father David had done. There were 153,600 of them.
\v 18 Solomon required 70,000 of them to carry heavy loads and 80,000 of them to cut stones from quarries in the hills. He appointed 3,600 other men to supervise them so that they would work steadily.
\c 3
\p
\v 1 Then Solomon’s workers started to build a temple for Yahweh in Jerusalem. They built it on Mount Moriah, where Yahweh had appeared to Solomon’s father David. They built it at the place where Araunah the Jebusite used to thresh his grain. David had chosen that place as the site for the temple.
\v 2 They started the work on the second day of the second month in the fourth year of Solomon’s reign.
\p
\v 3 The foundation of the temple that Solomon was building for God was about twenty-seven meters long and about nine meters wide.
\v 4 There was a porch in front of the temple that went across its entire width, so it was also about nine meters wide. This porch was about nine meters high. The workers covered the interior walls of the temple with very thin sheets of pure gold.
\p
\v 5 Solomon’s workers paneled the walls of the main hall of the temple with cypress wood. They decorated the panels with carvings of palm trees and chains. They covered the panels with very thin sheets of pure gold.
\v 6 The workers decorated the temple with gemstones to make it beautiful. The gold that they used came from the land of Parvaim.
\p
\v 7 They covered the ceiling beams, doorframes, walls and doors of the temple with very thin sheets of gold. They decorated the walls with artistic carvings of winged creatures.
\v 8 The workers built a room inside the temple to be the Most Holy Place. This room went all the way across the temple building, so it was about nine meters wide, and it extended out nine meters from the back wall of the temple. The workers covered its walls with very thin sheets of pure gold. Altogether this gold weighed about twenty metric tons.
\v 9 They used gold nails that weighed about half a kilogram. There were upper rooms in the temple, and the workers also covered their walls with very thin sheets of gold.
\p
\v 10 Solomon’s workers made wooden statues of two winged creatures to put inside the Most Holy Place. They covered them with very thin sheets of gold.
\v 11-12 Each creature had two wings. Each wing was about two and one third meters long, so the total length of all the wings was about nine meters. The workers arranged the statues so that each of the creatures’ outer wings touched a side wall of the room and their inner wings touched each other.
\p
\v 13 So the wings of these creatures extended all the way across the room, a distance of about nine meters. The creatures were standing up and facing the doorway leading to the main hall of the temple.
\p
\v 14 Solomon’s workers made a curtain {to separate the main hall from the Most Holy Place}. They wove it from blue, purple, red, and linen thread. They wove figures of winged creatures into the curtain.
\v 15 Huram Abi made two bronze pillars for the entrance to the temple. Their combined length was nearly 17 meters. Each pillar had a decorative top that was two and one third meters high.
\v 16 Huram made bronze chains like the ones inside the temple and put them on the decorative tops of the pillars. He made 100 bronze pomegranates for each pillar and attached them to the chains.
\v 17 Solomon”s workers set up the pillars in front of the temple. One was on the south side of the entrance and the other was on the north side. Solomon named the one on the south side Jakin, and he named the one on the north side Boaz.
\c 4
\p
\v 1 Solomon’s workers made a bronze altar. It was nine meters wide, nine meters deep, and four and and a half meters high.
\v 2 Huram Abi made a giant water basin. People called it a sea {because it contained so much water}. Huram made it by casting bronze in a clay mold. This sea was four and a half meters across its circular top, two and one third meters tall, and nearly 14 meters around.
\v 3 All around the outer edge of the sea there were decorations in the shape of oxen. There were two rows of oxen. There were ten oxen for every 45 centimeters of the outer edge. Hiram included the oxen as part of the design of the sea when he cast it.
\p
\v 4 Hiram cast 12 bronze statues of oxen to be a stand for the sea. He placed the oxen in groups of three facing outward to the north, south, east, and west. Then workers put the sea on the statues of the oxen, and it covered their backs.
\v 5 The sides of this large water basin were eight centimeters thick. When Hiram cast the basin, he made the rim curve outward, like the rim of a cup and like the petals of a lily. The basin could hold over 60,000 liters of water.
\p
\v 6 Huram also made ten basins. Solomon’s workers put five of these basins on the south side of the temple and five of them on the north side of the temple. They were for rinsing the parts of the animals that the priests burned completely on the altar as sacrifices. But the priests washed their hands and feet {to become ritually clean when they came to the temple} in the large water basin that people called a sea.
\p
\v 7 Huram also made ten gold lampstands in the same shape in which God had told Moses to make lampstands for the sacred tent. He put five of these lampstands on the south side in the temple and five of them on the north side of the temple.
\p
\v 8 Huram also made ten tables. He put five of them on the south side of the temple and five of them on the north side of the temple. He also made 100 gold basins.
\p
\v 9 Solomon’s workers constructed a courtyard for the priests and a larger court area for other Israelites. They made doors for the entrances to these courtyards, and they covered the doors with thin sheets of bronze.
\v 10 Solomon’s workers put the giant water basin that people called a sea by the southeast corner of the temple.
\p
\v 11 Huram also made {bronze} pots {in which to boil meat}, shovels {for removing ashes}, and bowls {to catch the blood of the animals that the priests sacrificed}.
\p So Huram finished doing the work that King Solomon had requested him to do for God’s temple.
\v 12 {What Huram made included} the two pillars {for the temple porch}, the two ball-shaped tops for the pillars, the two bronze nets that decorated the ball-shaped pillar tops,
\v 13 the 400 bronze pomegranates that decorated the two networks (these pomegranates were in two rows around each bronze net, and they covered the round lower parts of the pillar tops),
\q1 \v 14 ten carts and ten basins that the carts held,
\v 15 the giant water basin that people called the sea, the 12 statues of oxen on whose backs the sea rested,
\v 16 as well as pots, shovels, and meat forks.
\p Huram and his workers made all these things that King Solomon requested him to make for Yahweh’s temple. Hiram made them all from bronze, and his workers then polished them {so that they would gleam brightly}.
\v 17 Hiram and his workers made these things for Solomon in the Jordan River valley, between the cities of Succoth and Zaredathah. {They made them there because} the soil consisted of clay {that they could use to cast things in bronze}.
\v 18 Because Huram used so much bronze to make all these objects for Solomon, Solomon did not command his workers to weigh them. So no one ever knew what they weighed.
\p
\v 19 Solomon’s workers also made all the gold items that the priests needed to use in Yahweh’s temple. They made the golden altar and the table that held the sacred bread.
\v 20 They made from pure gold the lampstands and the lamps they held. They put the lampstands in front of the Most Holy Place. The priests kept those lamps burning all night long, as God had told Moses they should do.
\v 21 They also made decorations for the lampstands that looked like flowers, the lamps themselves, and tongs {for removing burnt parts of the lamp wicks}. They made all of these articles from solid gold.
\v 22 Solomon’s workers also made from pure gold the wick snuffers, small bowls, incense dishes, and pans for carrying hot coals {that the priests used in the temple}. They also made golden sockets for the doors of the temple building and for the doors inside the building that led into the Most Holy Place.
\c 5
\p
\v 1 So Solomon’s workers finished all the work for Yahweh’s temple. Then Solomon put the silver and gold and other valuable items that his father David had dedicated to Yahweh in the temple storerooms.
\p
\v 2 Solomon then sent messengers to tell the Israelite elders, tribal leaders, and clan leaders to come to Jerusalem. He wanted them to join him in a ceremony to move Yahweh’s sacred chest from the City of David (the part of Jerusalem that people now call Zion) {into the temple that he had built}.
\v 3 King Solomon asked the leaders to come during the Festival of Shelters, which the Israelites observed in the seventh month of the year. So a very large crowd of Israelites also came to Jerusalem at that time to celebrate this festival.
\p
\v 4 When the Israelite leaders had all arrived, the Levites carried the sacred chest {from the City of David}
\v 5 and brought it into the temple. The Levites {who assisted the priests} helped them carry into the temple the sacred tent and all the special equipment that the priests used only in the tent.
\v 6 King Solomon and that large crowd of Israelites walked together ahead of Yahweh’s sacred chest {as the priests carried it to the temple}. They sacrificed a very large number of sheep and bulls. No one was able to count how many animals they sacrificed, because there were so many.
\p
\v 7 The priests carried the sacred chest into the temple and put it where it belonged. That was in the Most Holy Place, the special room that Solomon’s workers had made at the back of the temple. They placed the sacred chest under the wings of the statues of the winged creatures there.
\v 8 Then the wings of those statues extended over the sacred chest and over the poles that the priests used to carry it.
\v 9 Those poles were so long that priests who were standing in the Holy Place at the entrance to the Most Holy Place could see their ends. But people standing outside the temple could not see them. Those poles are still there.
\v 10 The only things that were in the sacred chest were the two stone tablets that Moses had put there at Mount Horeb. That was where Yahweh had made a special agreement with the Israelites after they left Egypt.
\p
\v 11 Then the priests came out of the Holy Place{, after putting the sacred chest in the temple}. All the priests who were there had made themselves ritually clean, not just the ones from the group that was officially on duty that day.)
\v 12 The Levites who were musicians—Asaph, Heman, Jeduthun, and their sons and other relatives—were standing on the east side of the altar. They were wearing linen clothes, and they were playing cymbals, lyres, and harps. There were 120 priests with them who were playing trumpets.
\v 13 On their trumpets, the priests were accompanying the tune that the singers were singing. They were all praising and thanking Yahweh. The priests were playing their trumpets, and others were sounding cymbals and playing other musical instruments, as the Levites praised Yahweh by singing,
\q1 “He always does good things for us,
\q2 and he will love us faithfully forever!”
\m Suddenly a cloud filled the temple.
\v 14 The glorious presence of Yahweh, which the cloud expressed, filled the temple. As a result, the priests were not able to continue their work.
\c 6
\p
\v 1 Then Solomon {looked at the temple and} said, “Yahweh said that he would live in a dark cloud.”
\v 2 {Then Solomon prayed to Yahweh,} “But I have built a temple for you that you will always be able to live in.”
\p
\v 3 Then, while all the people stood there {respectfully}, the king turned around and faced them, and he asked God to make good things happen for them.
\v 4 Then he said, “Praise Yahweh, the God whom we Israelites worship! By his own power he has done what he promised to my father David. Yahweh told him,
\v 5 ‘I rescued the people who belong to me from slavery in Egypt. But right after that, I did not choose a city anywhere in their land and ask them to build a temple there where they would worship me. I also did not choose a man to be the king of my Israelite people.
\v 6 But now I have chosen Jerusalem as the place where people will worship me, and I have chosen you, David, to be the king of my Israelite people.’”
\v 7 Then Solomon said, “My father David wanted very much to build a temple where we Israelite people could worship Yahweh our God.
\v 8 But Yahweh told him, ‘You wanted to build a temple for me, and it was good that you wanted to do that.
\v 9 However, you are not the one who is going to build it. Instead, one of your own sons will build a temple where the Israelites will worship me.’
\v 10 And now Yahweh has done what he promised to do. I have succeeded my father David as the king of Israel, just as Yahweh promised. And I have built a temple where we Israelite people can worship Yahweh our God.
\v 11 I have provided a place within the temple for the sacred chest. It holds the two stone tablets that Moses put in it when Yahweh made a special agreement with our ancestors.”
\p
\v 12 Then Solomon turned around again and faced the altar {in the temple courtyard}. The Israelite people who had gathered for this occasion could see and hear him. He lifted his hands toward the sky {to pray}.
\v 13 Now Solomon’s workers had built a bronze platform for him. It was two and one third meters wide, two and one third meters deep, and one and a half meters high. They had put it in the courtyard outside the temple. Solomon had been standing on that platform, but then he knelt down in front of all the Israelites who had gathered there, and that was when he lifted his hands toward the sky.
\v 14 Then he prayed, “Yahweh, you are the God whom we Israelite people worship. There is no god like you in heaven or on earth. You solemnly promised that you would faithfully love us. And that is what you have always done for us when we have earnestly done what you wanted us to do.
\v 15 You promised my father David, who served you faithfully, that his son would build a temple for you. And we here today can all see that by your own power you have done what you promised.
\v 16 So now, Yahweh, the God whom we Israelites worship, I am asking you to do the further things you promised to my father David, who served you faithfully. You told him that you would make sure that one of his descendants would always be the king of Israel if they would conduct their lives as he did. He obeyed you carefully.
\v 17 You, Yahweh, are the God whom we Israelite people worship. So now please do what you promised to do for my father David.
\v 18 But you, God, will not really live with people here on the earth. You are so great that the whole sky and even the spiritual realm beyond the sky are not a sufficient home for you. So this temple that I have built is certainly not sufficient for you to live in either.
\v 19 Even so, Yahweh, my God, please grant the requests that I make as I pray to you today. I have come to your temple today to pray. Please do what I am earnestly asking you to do.
\v 20 I pray that you will always regard this temple favorably. You said that when we Israelites built a temple, you would be pleased to have people worship you there. So as I face this temple {to acknowledge you} as I pray, please answer my prayer.
\v 21 When I pray to you, or when Israelite people pray to you, we will face this temple {to acknowledge you}. You will be faraway in heaven where you live, but please do what we ask and forgive us {for the sins that we have committed}.
\p
\v 22 Suppose one person accuses another person of doing something wrong to him. Suppose the first person makes the other person stand in front of your altar outside this temple and swear, ‘I did not do that. May God punish me if I am not telling the truth!’
\v 23 In that case, please pay attention in heaven and decide which of these people is telling the truth. Then punish the person who is guilty with the punishment that he deserves, and reward the person who is innocent for having done what is right.
\p
\v 24 Suppose that the enemies of your Israelite people defeat their army in a battle because the Israelites have sinned against you. And suppose that the Israelites then admit that they have done wrong by sinning and that you are right to punish them for sinning. Suppose they stop acting in a sinful way. And suppose they face in the direction of this temple {to acknowledge you} and plead with you to help them.
\v 25 In that case, please pay attention in heaven, and please forgive your Israelite people for the sins that they have committed. Bring back to this land that you gave to our ancestors any prisoners whom the enemy army may have captured and taken away.
\p
\v 26 Suppose that your Israelite people sin against you and, to punish them, you do not allow any rain to fall from the sky. Suppose that, because you are punishing them in this way, they face in the direction of this temple {to acknowledge you}, and they pray. Suppose that they admit that you are right to punish them for sinning. Suppose they stop acting in a sinful way.
\v 27 In that case, {by not allowing rain to fall,} you would have taught them that they needed to live differently. So then please pay attention in heaven to their prayer, and forgive your Israelite people for the sins they have committed. Then send rain on this land that you have decided will belong permanently to your people.
\p
\v 28 Suppose the people of Israel are not able to grow enough food to eat. Or suppose a deadly disease spreads among the people. Or suppose blight or mildew ruin their crops or locusts or grasshoppers eat up their crops. Or suppose a person is in great distress because an enemy army has surrounded the city where he lives and is attacking it. Or suppose a person gets sick, or many people get sick.
\v 29 And suppose an Israelite person recognizes that he is suffering because you are correcting him for sinning. Suppose such a person pleads with you to help him. Suppose he stretches out his hands toward this temple {to acknowledge you}.
\v 30 In that case, please pay attention to his prayer in heaven where you live. If he is truly sorry, forgive him and help him. You are the only one who knows whether a person is truly sorry. So act toward each person as he deserves.
\v 31 Do this so that the Israelites will deeply respect you and obey you throughout their lives as they live in this land that you gave to our ancestors.
\p
\v 32 Suppose a foreigner who does not belong to your Israelite people comes from a faraway country because he has heard what a great God you are and what powerful things you have done for your people. Foreigners may come to this temple to {worship you and} pray to you.
\v 33 In that case, please pay attention to their prayers in heaven where you live. Do for them what they ask you to do. Do that so that all the people groups in the world will know how great you are and revere you as we, your Israelite people, do. Then people throughout the world will know that you are truly present in this temple that I have built.
\p
\v 34 Suppose that you command your people to go and fight against one of their enemies. And suppose that the Israelite soldiers pray to you {for help}. Suppose that{, to acknowledge you,} they face in the direction of this city of Jerusalem, which you have chosen as the place for this temple that I have built where we Israelites can worship you.
\v 35 In that case, please pay attention in heaven to their prayers for help, and enable them to defeat their enemies.
\p
\v 36 Suppose that your people sin against you, as everyone unfortunately does, and you become angry with them. Suppose that {to punish them} you allow one of their enemies to defeat them and take many of them away as prisoners to their own country, perhaps even to a country that is far away.
\v 37 And suppose that, while your people are in that country to which their enemy took them as captives, they sincerely repent. Suppose they plead with you there and say, ‘We confess that we have sinned by doing very wicked things.’
\v 38 Suppose that they start obeying you entirely again while they are still in the land of the enemy who captured them. Suppose that they pray to you for help while {to acknowledge you} they face in the direction of this land that you gave to our ancestors. Suppose they face this city of Jerusalem, which you have chosen as the place for this temple that I have built where we Israelites can worship you.
\v 39 In that case, please pay attention to their prayers for help in heaven where you live, and please rescue them. Please forgive them for the sins that they committed against you.
\p
\v 40 Oh my God, please regard us favorably and pay careful attention when we pray to you in this place.
\v 41 Yahweh our God, please come and stay in this place with the sacred chest through which you have acted so powerfully. Yahweh God, may your priests live holy lives so that they can help the people who are faithful to you experience good things that make them rejoice.
\v 42 Yahweh God, please always help me, the king whom you have appointed. Please remember how you promised King David, who served you well, that you would always love him faithfully.”
\c 7
\p
\v 1 When Solomon finished his prayer, fire came down from the sky. It burned up the animals that the people had sacrificed and the other offerings. Yahweh’s glory filled the temple.
\v 2 Because Yahweh’s glory had filled the temple, the priests could not go inside it.
\v 3 The Israelite people who were there saw the fire coming down and Yahweh’s glory over the temple. They bowed down with their faces touching the stone pavement. They worshiped and thanked Yahweh, saying,
\q1 “He always does good things for us,
\q2 and he will faithfully love us forever!”
\m
\v 4 Then King Solomon and all the Israelites who had come to Jerusalem for the temple dedication offered sacrifices to Yahweh.
\v 5 They sacrificed 22,000 cattle and 120,000 sheep as fellowship sacrifices to Yahweh. The king and all the Israelites who were there did that to dedicate God’s temple.
\v 6 The priests stood in their regular positions. The Levites took their places with the musical instruments that King David had made for praising Yahweh. (David had played those instruments himself to praise Yahweh.) They sang,
\q1 “He loves us faithfully forever!”
\m The priests were playing their trumpets to accompany them, and the other Israelites who were there were all standing up respectfully.
\p
\v 7 The bronze altar that Solomon had made was not large enough for all of the sacrifices that the priests would burn completely that day. It also could not hold all the grain offerings and the fat from the fellowship sacrifices. So Solomon made the middle part of the courtyard, in front of the temple, a holy place for that day. That was where the priests burned sacrifices completely and burned the fat from the fellowship sacrifices.
\p
\v 8 Solomon and all the Israelites who had come to Jerusalem then celebrated the Festival of Shelters. They celebrated it for seven days. There was a huge crowd of people there who came from many different places in the land of Israel.
\v 9 On the day after the Festival of Shelters ended, they all gathered together again to worship Yahweh. They had celebrated the dedication of the temple for seven days and the Festival of Shelters for seven days.
\v 10 The day after that, Solomon told the people they could go back to their homes. (By then it was the 23rd day of the seventh month of their year.) As the people went home, they were very happy because of the good things that Yahweh had done for David and Solomon and the Israelite people, who belonged to him.
\p
\v 11 After Solomon’s workers had finished building everything that Solomon wanted them to build for the temple and for his royal palace just as he wanted it,
\v 12 Yahweh appeared to him {in a dream} one night. Yahweh told him, “I agree to do what you asked me to do when you prayed to me. I have made this temple a place where I will be present in a special way so that people can always worship me there.
\p
\v 13 Suppose that{, to punish the Israelites for sinning,} I do not allow any rain to fall from the sky. Or suppose that I allow locusts to eat up your crops. Or suppose that I allow a deadly disease to spread among the Israelites.
\v 14 But suppose that the Israelites, who belong to me, become very sorry for their sins and stop sinning. Suppose they ask me to forgive them and they try to do what pleases me. Then I, in heaven, will pay attention to what they ask. I will forgive their sins, and I will make it rain again or stop the locusts from eating their crops or prevent the disease from spreading further.
\v 15 Then I will regard them favorably, and I will pay careful attention when they pray to me in this place.
\v 16 I will make the temple you have built a place where I will be always present in a special way. I will always pay attention to what people pray when they face the temple, and I will always care deeply about what happens there.
\p
\v 17 And as for you, conduct your life as I want you to, just as your father David did. Obey very sincerely all the laws that I have commanded you to obey {as an Israelite}. If you do that,
\v 18 then I will make sure that you and your descendants are kings of Israel. That is the promise I made to your father David. I told him that one of his descendants would always rule Israel.
\p
\v 19 But suppose that you or one of your descendants who becomes king stops worshiping me. Suppose you disobey the laws that I have given to you to obey. Suppose that you start to live as people live who worship other gods. Suppose you even bow down to idols that represent those gods.
\v 20 If you do that, then I will allow an enemy to take the Israelite people away from this land that I have given to them. I will also allow an enemy to destroy this temple that I have made a place where I will be especially present. Then people throughout the world will use the Israelites as an example of a very bad thing happening to a nation. They will even mock other people by saying that they are like the Israelites.
\v 21 People who see this temple now consider it very beautiful. But enemies will destroy it. People will ask, ‘Why did Yahweh allow enemies to take the Israelite people away from this land and destroy this temple?’
\v 22 Other people will reply, ‘This happened because the Israelite people abandoned Yahweh, the God whom their ancestors worshiped. He was the one who rescued them from slavery in Egypt. But they became loyal to other gods instead. They lived the way people who worship those other gods live. They even bowed down to idols that represented those gods. That is why Yahweh caused them to experience all these disasters.’”
\c 8
\p
\v 1 Solomon’s workers spent 20 years building Yahweh’s temple and Solomon’s palace. After that,
\v 2 his workers rebuilt the cities that King Huram had given to him. Solomon settled Israelites in those cities.
\v 3 Solomon’s army then attacked the city of Hamath Zobah and captured it.
\v 4 Then Solomon’s workers built up the city of Tadmor in the desolate area in the southern part of Judah. They also built cities in the region of Hamath where Solomon kept supplies.
\v 5 They also built up as border fortress cities Upper Beth Horon and Lower Beth Horon. They built strong walls around them that had gates that people could close with metal bars.
\v 6 They also built up the city of Baalath and all the cities where Solomon kept supplies and the places where he kept his chariots and horses. They also built everything else that Solomon wanted them to build in Jerusalem, Lebanon, and other places where he had royal authority.
\p
\v 7 There were many people still living in Solomon’s kingdom who were not Israelites. Rather, they were Hittites, Amorites, Perizzites, Hivites, and Jebusites.
\v 8 These people were descendants of members of those people groups who remained in the land of Canaan after the Israelites had destroyed most of their groups. The Israelites had not killed their ancestors. It was those people whom Solomon forced to work hard on his building projects. The Israelites still require those people to work for them.
\v 9 But Solomon did not force any Israelite people to work hard on his building projects. Instead, some of them became his soldiers, army officers, and leaders of his chariot forces and troops who rode on horses.
\v 10 King Solomon also had 250 officials who supervised the men who commanded the workers whom he forced to build all those places.
\p
\v 11 One of Solomon’s wives was a daughter of the king of Egypt. She had been living in the part of Jerusalem that people call the City of David. But Solomon built a palace for her in another part of Jerusalem. He felt that because she was a foreigner, she should not live in a place where the sacred chest had been {before he moved it to the temple}. When David was the king of Israel, he had set up a special tent for the chest on the grounds of his palace, and Solomon felt that this made those grounds holy. So he moved his wife to another place.
\p
\v 12 Then Solomon offered sacrifices to Yahweh that the priests burned completely on the altar that he had built for Yahweh. That altar was in front of the temple porch.
\v 13 He did that to obey what Moses had commanded regarding what sacrifices the Israelites should offer at various times. Those times included Sabbath days, New Moon festivals, and the feasts that the law commanded the Israelites to celebrate three times each year. Those were the Festival of Unleavened Bread, the Celebration of Weeks, and the Festival of Shelters.
\v 14 As his father David had instructed, Solomon appointed the groups of priests to do their work. He also appointed the groups of Levites to sing to praise Yahweh and to assist the priests in their daily work. He also appointed groups of Levites to guard each of the gates of the temple. That was what David, that godly man, had commanded.
\v 15 The people obeyed everything that the king had commanded about the priests and Levites, including his command that they should take care of the temple storerooms.
\p
\v 16 Solomon’s workers accomplished everything Solomon told them to do to build Yahweh’s temple, from laying its foundation to finishing its last details. And so they completed the construction of Yahweh’s temple.
\p
\v 17 Then Solomon sent some of his men to the cities of Ezion Geber and Eloth on the coast of the sea in the land of Edom.
\v 18 Some of King Huram’s subjects knew well how to sail in the ocean. He had them sail some ships to where Solomon’s men were. They sailed together to the region of Ophir. From there, they brought back about 15 metric tons of gold to King Solomon.
\c 9
\p
\v 1 The queen who ruled the land of Sheba heard that Solomon had great understanding. She wanted to know whether what she had heard about him was true. So she traveled to Jerusalem to ask him many difficult questions. She brought many of her officials with her in a large caravan. The camels were carrying spices, much gold, and gemstones {as gifts for Solomon}. Solomon welcomed her, and she asked him about everything she wanted to know.
\v 2 Solomon answered all of her questions. Not one of her questions was too difficult for him to answer.
\p
\v 3 The Queen recognized that Solomon was very wise. She toured his palace,
\v 4 and she saw the food that he served. She saw how his many officials sat in different places of honor at meals. She saw how the waiters and cupbearers who served the meals wore splendid uniforms. She also saw the many animals that he brought to the temple for the priests to burn completely as sacrifices. What she saw overwhelmed her.
\p
\v 5 She told King Solomon, “Everything that I heard back in my own country about how wisely you speak is true!
\v 6 But I did not believe it was true until I came here and saw it for myself. Indeed, what people told me was only half of what they could have told me about how very wise you are. You are much greater than people told me.
\v 7 How fortunate are your subjects! And how fortunate are your servants, who are always standing around you and listening to the wise things that you say!
\v 8 I praise Yahweh, your God, who has shown that he is pleased with you by allowing you to serve him as the king of Israel! Your God loves the Israelite people and wants them always to be a nation. That is why he has appointed you to be a king who will rule them fairly and righteously.”
\p
\v 9 The Queen of Sheba gave King Solomon {as gifts from what she had brought} over 4, 000 kilograms of gold, many gemstones, and a very large amount of spices. The spices that she gave to Solomon were the best that anyone ever brought to Jerusalem.
\p
\v 10-12 The Queen of Sheba admired many things in Jerusalem and asked for them, and King Solomon gave them to her. He gave her more than she had given to him. Then she and the officials who had come with her returned to their own land.
\p When King Hiram’s sailors took King Solomon’s men to Ophir, they brought back gold. They also brought back sandalwood and gemstones. King Solomon had his workers use that wood to make steps for the temple and for his palace. He also had them use it to make harps and lyres for the temple musicians. Never before had anyone brought such sandalwood to Judah.
\p
\v 13 Each year Solomon received over 22 metric tons of gold in tribute from kingdoms that he controlled.
\v 14 In addition to that, merchants and traders who traveled through and within Israel paid him duties and tolls. Arabian kings also often sent him gifts of gold and silver, and the district governors submitted the taxes they collected in gold or silver.
\p
\v 15 King Solomon told his workers to take gold and hammer it {into thin sheets and cover} 200 large shields with {those thin sheets of} gold. They used nearly seven kilograms of gold to make each large shield.
\v 16 He also told his workers to cover 300 smaller shields with gold. They covered each of them with about one and a half kilograms of gold. King Solomon put all of those shields in the House of the Forest of Lebanon.
\p
\v 17 He also had his workers make a large throne for him. The workers inlaid this throne with ivory, and they framed the ivory with borders of very fine gold.
\v 18 There were six steps in front of the throne. The workers attached a gold footstool to the throne. There was an armrest on each side of the throne. There was a statue of a lion next to each armrest.
\v 19 There was also a statue of a lion on both sides of each step. So altogether there were 12 statues of lions. There was no throne like this one in any other kingdom.
\v 20 King Solomon and his court officials drank only from gold cups. His craftsmen also made all of the dishes for the House of the Forest of Lebanon by hammering out gold. (Silver was so common during the time when Solomon ruled that people did not consider it to be valuable.)
\v 21 They had so much gold because King Solomon had a fleet of ships that were able to sail out on the sea. King Huram’s men sailed them. The ships would sail to many different ports for three years. At the end of that time, they would bring back gold, silver, ivory, monkeys, and peacocks.
\p
\v 22 King Solomon became the richest and wisest king in the world.
\v 23 Kings from many different places came and listened to the wise things that Solomon said about matters that God had enabled him to understand.
\v 24 Each of the people who came to him brought presents. They brought articles made from silver or gold, or robes, or weapons, or spices, or horses, or mules. The people continued to do this every year.
\p
\v 25 Solomon had 4,000 stalls where he kept the horses that pulled his chariots. He had 12,000 soldiers who rode on horses. Solomon had built special cities in which to keep chariots, and he kept many of his chariots in them. But he also kept some chariots with him in Jerusalem.
\v 26 Solomon became the ruler of all the kings in the area from the Euphrates River {in the northeast} to the region of Philistia {in the west} and to the border of Egypt {in the south}.
\v 27 During the years that Solomon was king, silver became very common in Jerusalem, just as stones are common. Cedar wood became very plentiful, just as the wood is plentiful that comes from the sycamore trees that grow in the foothills of Judah.
\v 28 Solomon’s purchasing agents would go to Egypt and to many other countries to buy horses for him.
\p
\v 29 Several books record further things that Solomon did throughout his reign. These books include the records of the prophets Nathan and Ahijah the Shilonite and the accounts by the prophet Iddo of the visions that he saw regarding Jeroboam son of Nebat.
\v 30 Solomon reigned in Jerusalem over all of Israel for forty years.
\v 31 Then Solomon died. The Israelites buried him in the part of Jerusalem that people called the City of David. David had been his father. Solomon’s son Rehoboam became the next king.
\c 10
\p
\v 1 Leaders from all the tribes of Israel went to the city of Shechem in order to appoint Rehoboam as their king. So Rehoboam also went there.
\v 2 Jeroboam son of Nebat had fled to Egypt to escape from King Solomon. But when he learned that Solomon had died, he came back from Egypt.
\v 3 The Israelite leaders asked Jeroboam to come to Shechem, and they went there together to speak with Rehoboam. They told him,
\v 4 “Your father Solomon forced us to work very hard, but if you allow us to work less hard and allow us to pay fewer taxes, then we will agree to be your subjects.”
\p
\v 5 Rehoboam replied, “Go away for a couple of days {to give me some time to consider that}. Then come back {and I will give you my answer}.” So those leaders and Jeroboam left.
\p
\v 6 Then King Rehoboam asked for advice from the older men who had been officials of his father Solomon while he was still living. He asked them, “What do you think I should say in response to what these Israelite leaders have asked me to do?”
\p
\v 7 They replied, “We recommend that you be kind to them and treat them favorably. If you speak kindly to them when you reply to them, they will always be your loyal subjects.”
\p
\v 8 But Rehoboam decided not to do what the older men had advised him to do. Instead, he asked for advice from the younger men who had been his friends since they were boys and who were now his advisors.
\v 9 He asked them, “What do you think we should say in response to these Israelite leaders who have asked me to allow them to work less hard than my father required them to work?”
\p
\v 10 Those young men who were his boyhood friends replied, “This is what you should tell these people who asked you to allow them to work less hard than your father required them to work. You should say, ‘I am a harsher man than my father was.
\v 11 This is my answer to you. My father required you to work hard. But I am going to make you work even harder. My father had his overseers discipline you Israelite workers harshly. But I am going to have them discipline you even more harshly.’”
\p
\v 12 A couple of days after Jeroboam and the Israelite leaders first met with Rehoboam, they came to speak with him again, just as he had told them to do.
\v 13 The king disregarded the advice that the older men had given him. Instead, he spoke roughly to the Israelite leaders.
\v 14 He told them what the younger men had advised him to say. He said, “I am going to make you work even harder than my father required you to work. My father had his overseers discipline you Israelite workers harshly. But I am going to have them discipline you even more harshly.”
\v 15 So the king did not do what the Israelite leaders wanted him to do. That was because God caused him to refuse their request. In that way, Yahweh made happen what he had told Jeroboam son of Nebat through the prophet Ahijah the Shilonite.
\p
\v 16 When the Israelite leaders realized that Rehoboam was not going to do what they had asked, they responded to him by shouting,
\q1 “We do not want to be subjects of this descendant of King David!
\q2 We will not accept this grandson of Jesse as our ruler!
\q1 We Israelites should all go home!
\q2 As for this descendant of David, he can rule his own tribe!”
\m So the Israelite leaders returned to their homes.
\p
\v 17 (After that, the only Israelites who accepted Rehoboam as their king were the members of the tribe of Judah.)
\p
\v 18 King Rehoboam had an official whose name was Hadoram. He supervised the men whom Rehoboam forced to work for him. {Before the Israelite leaders left,} King Rehoboam sent Hadoram out {to try to make them obey him}. But the Israelite people killed him by throwing stones at him. When that happened, King Rehoboam got into his chariot as fast as he could and escaped to Jerusalem.
\v 19 Ever since that time, the people of the northern tribes of Israel have refused to be subjects of descendants of King David.
\c 11
\p
\v 1 When Rehoboam got back to Jerusalem, he gathered 180,000 of the best soldiers from the tribes of Judah and Benjamin. He wanted them to fight against soldiers of the northern Israelite tribes {and defeat them} so that he could rule all of the Israelite tribes again.
\p
\v 2 But Yahweh spoke to the prophet Shemaiah and told him,
\v 3 “Here is something that I want you to tell Rehoboam son of Solomon, the king of Judah, and all the Israelite people of the tribes of Judah and Benjamin.
\v 4 Tell them that I say that they must not go to fight against their fellow Israelites. They must all go home. Tell them that this has happened because I wanted it to happen.” {So Shemaiah went and told that to them,} and they all obeyed what Yahweh had commanded them to do. They did not attack the other Israelites.
\p
\v 5 Rehoboam lived in Jerusalem, and he had his workers build walls around several cities in Judah.
\v 6 They built walls around Bethlehem, Etam, Tekoa,
\v 7 Beth Zur, Soko, Adullam,
\v 8 Gath, Mareshah, Ziph,
\v 9 Adoraim, Lachish, Azekah,
\v 10 Zorah, Aijalon, and Hebron. All of these cities around which the workers built walls were within the territory of the tribes of Judah and Benjamin.
\v 11 Once these cities had strong walls around them, Rehoboam appointed an army commander in each one. He gave them supplies of food, olive oil, and wine.
\v 12 He provided large shields and spears for soldiers to use in each of those cities. These arrangements made the cities very difficult to conquer. So Rehoboam continued to rule the tribes of Judah and Benjamin.
\p
\v 13 Priests and Levites who lived in many places throughout Israel came to Rehoboam and asked to work in his kingdom.
\v 14 Those descendants of Levi abandoned their property and their pastureland and moved to Jerusalem and to other places in Judah. They did that because Jeroboam{, who had become the king of the other tribes,} and his sons would not let them lead or support the worship of Yahweh.
\v 15 Instead, Jeroboam appointed priests {other than the ones the law of Moses allowed}. He had them lead worship at hilltop shrines and at places where people adored the goat idols and calf statues that he had made.)
\v 16 Then people from every tribe in Israel who wanted to worship Yahweh, the God of the Israelites, came to Jerusalem to offer sacrifices to Yahweh, the God whom their ancestors had worshiped.
\v 17 This made the kingdom of Judah strong, and it made Rehoboam son of Solomon a powerful king, for the next three years. That was because, during that time, the people of his kingdom faithfully worshiped Yahweh as David and Solomon had.
\p
\v 18 Rehoboam married a woman whose name was Mahalath. She was the daughter of David’s son Jerimoth. Her mother was Abihail, the daughter of Eliab and granddaughter of Jesse.
\v 19 Rehoboam and Mahalath had three sons: Jeush, Shemariah, and Zaham.
\v 20 Later Rehoboam married Maacah, the daughter of Absalom, and they had four sons: Abijah, Attai, Ziza, and Shelomith.
\v 21 Rehoboam loved Maacah more than he loved any other of his wives or concubines. Altogether he had 18 wives and 60 concubines, and with them he had 28 sons and 60 daughters.
\p
\v 22 Rehoboam appointed Maacah’s son Abijah to be the crown prince among his older and younger brothers. He did that because he wanted Abijah to be the next king.
\v 23 Rehoboam wisely sent some of his other sons to various cities that had walls around them within the territories of the tribes of Judah and Benjamin. He made sure that these cities had plenty of supplies. He arranged marriages for each of those sons.
\c 12
\p
\v 1 But once Rehoboam had become a secure and powerful king, he and other people in Judah stopped obeying Yahweh’s laws.
\v 2 As a result, after Rehoboam had been king for almost five years, Shishak, the king of Egypt, came with his army to attack Jerusalem. That happened because the Judeans were no longer loyal to Yahweh.
\v 3 Shishak brought 1,200 chariots and 60,000 soldiers who rode horses. He also brought more Libyan, Sukkite, and Cushite troops with him from Egypt than anyone could count.
\v 4 They captured the other cities in Judah that had walls around them. Then they started attacking Jerusalem.
\p
\v 5 Many Judean leaders had fled to Jerusalem because they were afraid of Shishak and his army. The prophet Shemaiah came and said to them and to King Rehoboam, “Yahweh says that because you have abandoned him, now he is abandoning you. He is going to allow Shishak and his army to capture you.”
\p
\v 6 Then the king and the other Israelite leaders became very sorry for what they had done. They admitted, “What Yahweh is doing to us is fair.”
\p
\v 7 When Yahweh recognized that they were sorry for what they had done, he gave another message to Shemaiah. He told him, “Because they are sorry, I will not destroy them. I will allow some Judeans to survive. I will not use Shishak’s army to destroy the people of Jerusalem, even though they have made me very angry.
\v 8 However, they will become Shishak’s subjects. Then the Judeans will learn that it is much better to serve me than to serve the kings of other countries.”
\p
\v 9 When King Shishak of Egypt came with his soldiers to attack Jerusalem{, he defeated the Judeans, and then} his soldiers took away the valuable things they found in Yahweh’s temple and in the royal palace. They also took many other valuable things that they found. Because they took the gold shields that Solomon’s workers had made,
\v 10 King Rehoboam had his workers make bronze shields to replace them. He had the officers of the soldiers who guarded the entrance to his palace take care of these shields.
\v 11 Whenever the king was going to come into the temple, those guards got those shields and held them, and {after he left the temple,} they returned the shields to their storeroom.
\p
\v 12 Because Rehoboam was sorry for what he had done, Yahweh stopped being angry with him. He allowed him to continue being the king, even though he was now much weaker. {Yahweh was also merciful because he recognized that} many people in Judah were still living as he wanted them to live.
\p
\v 13 King Rehoboam made himself a strong king once again in Jerusalem. He had been 41 years old when he became the king, and he ruled for 17 years in Jerusalem. That was the city that Yahweh chose from all the tribes in Israel as the place where people would worship him. Rehoboam’s mother was a woman whose name was Naamah. She was from the nation of Ammon.
\v 14 Rehoboam did not try to find out what Yahweh wanted him to do, so he did things that were wrong.
\p
\v 15 Some books record further things that Rehoboam did throughout his reign. These books include the records of the prophet Shemaiah and the records that the prophet Iddo maintained to preserve a register of the descendants of David. The armies of Rehoboam and Jeroboam were continually fighting each other.
\v 16 When Rehoboam died, people buried him in the part of Jerusalem that they called the City of David. His son Abijah became the next king.
\c 13
\p
\v 1 After Jeroboam had been ruling Israel for almost 18 years, Abijah became the king of Judah.
\v 2 He ruled in Jerusalem for three years. His mother was a woman whose name was Micaiah. She was the daughter of Uriel from the city of Gibeah.
\p The armies of Abijah and Jeroboam fought a war.
\v 3 Abijah led an army of 400,000 of his best soldiers into battle. Jeroboam formed a battle line of 800,000 of his best and bravest soldiers to oppose him.
\p
\v 4 Abijah went up on the top of Mount Zemaraim, which is in the hilly area where the descendants of Ephraim lived. He shouted, “Listen to me, Jeroboam and all you Israelite soldiers!
\v 5 You know well that Yahweh, the God of us Israelites, promised David that he and his descendants would always rule Israel. He made that as a permanent promise.
\v 6 But Jeroboam son of Nebat, who had been an official of Solomon son of David, rebelled against his king.
\v 7 That was when Rehoboam son of Solomon {had become king but} was still young and lacked experience. A group of worthless, evil men gathered around Jeroboam, and together they rebelled against him. He was not strong enough to oppose them.
\p
\v 8 And now you have decided to fight against the kingdom that Yahweh wants David’s descendants to govern. You have a huge army, and you have brought with you the golden statues of calves that Jeroboam has made to be your gods.
\v 9 But you have driven out the priests whom Yahweh appointed, who are descendants of Aaron. You have also driven out the Levites. You have chosen your own priests, as the people of other countries do. If someone wants to be a priest, all he has to do is sacrifice a young bull and seven rams. Then you let him be a priest. But he is a priest for idols that are not gods at all!
\p
\v 10 As for us, Yahweh is our God, and we have remained loyal to him. Our priests who serve Yahweh are descendants of Aaron, and the ones who assist them in their work are descendants of Levi.
\v 11 Every morning and every evening, they burn animals completely on the altar as sacrifices to Yahweh. They burn fragrant incense with them. Every week they place fresh bread in rows on the pure gold table {in Yahweh’s temple}. Every evening they light the lamps that are on the gold lampstand. So we are obeying what Yahweh our God has instructed us to do. But you no longer worship him.
\v 12 You can see our priests with their trumpets at the front of our army. God is with them. They will blow their trumpets to signal our soldiers to start fighting. {When they do,} you Israelite soldiers should surrender, because you will not be able to defeat Yahweh, the God to whom your ancestors belonged.”
\p
\v 13 {While Abijah was speaking,} Jeroboam sent some of his soldiers around to attack the army of Judah from the rear. So Jeroboam and many of his soldiers were in front of the army of Judah, but some of his other soldiers were behind them.
\v 14 The soldiers of Judah looked around and saw that the Israelite solders were going to attack them from the front and from the rear. They cried out to Yahweh for help. Then the priests blew their trumpets
\v 15 and the soldiers of Judah shouted a loud battle cry. When they did that, God enabled Abijah and the army of Judah to defeat Jeroboam and the army of Israel.
\v 16 God enabled the army of Judah to win the battle, and the soldiers of Israel fled from the soldiers of Judah.
\v 17 Abijah and his soldiers won a great victory over them. They killed 500,000 of the best Israelite soldiers.
\p
\v 18 The soldiers of Judah won the battle because they depended on Yahweh, the God to whom their ancestors belonged. They completely defeated the soldiers of Israel on that occasion.
\p
\v 19 Abijah’s soldiers chased Jeroboam’s army away, and they captured from the Israelites the cities of Bethel, Jeshanah, and Ephron, and their surrounding villages.
\v 20 Jeroboam did not become powerful again while Abijah was the king of Judah. Yahweh caused him to become very sick, and he died.
\p
\v 21 But Abijah became more powerful. He married 14 wives and had 22 sons and 16 daughters.
\p
\v 22 The record that the prophet Iddo made describes further things that Abijah did. It describes how he lived and things that he said.
\c 14
\p
\v 1 When Abijah died, the Judeans buried him in the part of Jerusalem that called the City of David. His son Asa became the next king. While Asa was ruling, the land of Judah was a peaceful place for ten years.
\p
\v 2 Asa did things that Yahweh his God considered to be good and right.
\v 3 He had his workers destroy the altars and hilltop shrines for worshiping foreign gods {that his grandfather Rehoboam had built}. He had them smash the stone pillars {that Rehoboam had set up} and cut down the wooden images that represented the goddess Asherah.
\v 4 Asa told the people of Judah to worship only Yahweh, the God whom their ancestors had worshiped. He told them to obey the commandments in his law.
\v 5 He had his workers destroy the idols representing the sun and the hilltop shrines throughout Judah. As a result, while Asa was the king, his kingdom was a peaceful place.
\v 6 No army attacked Judah during those years, because Yahweh kept them from attacking. This gave Asa the opportunity to build cities with strong walls around them throughout Judah.
\p
\v 7 Asa told the people of Judah, “We should protect these cities by building strong walls around them that have gates that people can close with metal bars. We should also build defensive towers. This land still belongs to us because we have resolved to worship only Yahweh our God. Because we have done that, he has prevented the enemy countries around us from attacking us.” The people of Judah succeeded in building walls and towers for their cities.
\p
\v 8 In Asa’s army, there were 300,000 soldiers from the tribe of Judah. Their equipment was large shields and spears. There were also 280,000 men from the tribe of Benjamin. Their equipment was smaller shields and bows and arrows. They were all brave soldiers.
\p
\v 9 After this, Zerah, the king of the Cushites, attacked Judah with 300 chariots and more soldiers than anyone could count. They marched to the city of Mareshah {southwest of Jerusalem}.
\v 10 Asa led his army to fight against them. The armies set up battle lines in the Valley of Zephathah near Mareshah.
\p
\v 11 Then Asa prayed out loud to Yahweh his God. He said, “Yahweh, it makes no difference to you whether a kingdom has a large army or a little one. You can help that kingdom either way. We are depending completely on you as our God, Yahweh, and we have come to fight against this huge army for the sake of your reputation. So please help us. Yahweh, you are our God. Do not allow any human force to defeat you.”
\p
\v 12 Then Yahweh enabled Asa and the Judean army to defeat the Cushite army. Its soldiers ran away,
\v 13 and Asa and his army pursued them as far as the city of Gerar. Yahweh enabled the Judeans to defeat them severely. So many of the Cushites died that it seemed as if none of them remained alive. The Judeans took away a very great amount of their valuable possessions.
\v 14 The people who lived in the cities around Gerar became very afraid of Yahweh. As a result, the Judeans were able to conquer all those cities. There were many valuable things in them, and the Judeans took them away.
\v 15 They also attacked the places where people took care of animals. They took away large flocks of sheep and goats, and they also captured many camels. Then the Judeans went back to Jerusalem.
\c 15
\p
\v 1 Then God’s Spirit influenced Azariah son of Oded.
\v 2 Azariah went to meet Asa. He said to him, “Listen to me, King Asa and all you soldiers from the tribes of Judah and Benjamin! Yahweh will help you when you are loyal to him. If you devote yourselves to him, he will let you have a relationship with him as your God. But if you worship some other god, he will not keep helping you.
\v 3 For a long time, the Israelites did not worship the true God. They did not have priests teaching them, so they did not obey God’s laws.
\v 4 But when they experienced trouble, they asked Yahweh, the God of the Israelites, to help them. They devoted themselves to him, and he let them have a relationship with him as their God.
\v 5 During that time, the people who lived in many countries were experiencing great difficulties, so people were not safe when they traveled.
\v 6 God was allowing people to experience many difficulties, so the army of one nation would destroy another nation, or the army of one city would destroy another city.
\v 7 But God will reward you Judeans for what you are doing {to please him}, so you must be strong and not lose courage.
\p
\v 8 When Asa heard what the prophet {Azariah son of} Oded said when he prophesied, he determined to act. Asa commanded his workers to remove the detestable idols from throughout the territory of the tribes of Judah and Benjamin. He also had them remove them from the cities that his soldiers had captured in the hilly area where the descendants of Ephraim lived. Asa also had his workers repair the altar on which priests offered sacrifices to Yahweh that was in front of the entrance to the temple in Jerusalem.
\v 9 Asa then called for people from throughout the tribes of Judah and Benjamin to come to Jerusalem. Many people from the tribes of Ephraim, Manasseh, and Simeon were living among them, and he called them to come too. Those people were there because they had left Israel and moved to Judah when they realized that Yahweh was helping Asa because Asa was loyal to him as his God.
\p
\v 10 So those people came to Jerusalem in the third month of the fifteenth year that Asa was the king.
\v 11 At that time, they sacrificed 700 bulls and 7,000 sheep to Yahweh. Those were animals that they had captured when they defeated the Cushite army.
\v 12 They made a solemn agreement to be wholeheartedly and enthusiastically loyal to Yahweh, the God whom their ancestors had worshiped.
\v 13 They agreed that they would execute any people who worshiped a different god. It would not matter how important those people were or whether they were men or women.
\v 14 They said loudly that they would keep this promise to Yahweh. Then people shouted and blew trumpets and ram’s horns {to celebrate}.
\v 15 The people had made this solemn agreement very sincerely. They had eagerly devoted themselves to Yahweh, and now they had a relationship with him as their God. So people throughout the kingdom of Judah were very happy about this agreement. After this, Yahweh kept the countries around them from attacking them.
\p
\v 16 King Asa’s grandmother Maacah had been in the influential position of queen mother, but he removed her from that position. He did that because she had made a disgusting wooden statue for the goddess Asherah. Asa had his servants cut down the statue, smash it, and burn it in the Kidron Valley.
\v 17 He did not order his servants to destroy the shrines for worship on the tops of hills that were in the land of Israel. Even so, Asa was completely faithful to Yahweh for the whole time that he lived.
\v 18 He ordered his workers to put in God’s temple the silver and gold and other valuable items that he and his father had dedicated.
\p
\v 19 Asa did not have to fight any more wars until after he had ruled Judah for 35 years.
\c 16
\p
\v 1 When Asa had been ruling Judah for almost 36 years, King Baasha of Israel and his army invaded Judah. They captured the city of Ramah {just north of Jerusalem}. Then they started to build a wall around it {so that it would be a border fortress and they could} prevent people from entering or leaving Asa’s kingdom of Judah.
\p
\v 2 So Asa had his officials collect the silver and golden articles from the storerooms in the temple and his palace. He had them bring those things to Damascus and give them to Ben Hadad, the king who ruled Aram from that city. Asa told his officials to tell Ben Hadad,
\v 3 “My father had an alliance with your father, and I would like to have an alliance with you. {To make that alliance}, I am giving you this silver and gold. So please break the alliance you have with Baasha, the king of Israel{, and attack him}. That way he will stop invading my kingdom {because he will have to send his army to fight your army}.”
\p
\v 4 Ben Hadad agreed to do what King Asa wanted. He ordered his army commanders to lead their soldiers to attack some areas in the kingdom of Israel. They attacked the cities of Ijon, Dan, and Abel Maim. They also captured all the cities where the people of the tribe of Naphtali kept their supplies.
\v 5 When Baasha learned that Ben Hadad’s armies were attacking Israel, he ordered his soldiers to stop building the wall around Ramah.
\v 6 Then King Asa sent messengers to gather men from throughout the kingdom of Judah. He made the men carry away the stones and timber that Baasha’s soldiers had been using to build a wall around Ramah. With those stones and timber, King Asa had his workers fortify the cities of Geba and Mizpah.
\p
\v 7 But then the prophet Hanani came to King Asa of Judah and told him, “You should have depended on Yahweh your God, not on the king of Aram You could have defeated the army of the king of Aram, but now you will not.
\v 8 Remember how the Cushites and Lubites attacked you with a huge army that had very many chariots and soldiers who rode horses. But you depended on Yahweh, and he enabled your army to defeat them.
\v 9 Yahweh did that because he looks all over the world to see who trusts him completely so that he can help those people powerfully. This time you have acted without regard for Yahweh. So from now on, you will have to fight wars.”
\p
\v 10 {When he heard that,} Asa became very angry with the prophet. He commanded his officials to put Hanani in prison and clamp a wooden frame around his ankles. He gave that command because he was so angry with Hanani because of what he had said. At that same time, Asa also treated some of his other subjects cruelly.
\p
\v 11 The book in which the kings of Judah and Israel recorded what happened during their reigns describes further things that Asa did throughout his reign.
\v 12 When Asa had been ruling for almost 39 years, he got a disease in his feet. The disease became very severe, but nevertheless he did not ask Yahweh to help him. Instead, he only asked doctors to help him.
\v 13 Asa died after he had been ruling for about 41 years.
\v 14 Asa had ordered his workers to carve tombs out of the rocky cliffs near the part of Jerusalem that people called the City of David. The Judeans buried him in one of those tombs. They put his body on a bier that they covered with spices and various ointments that perfumers had prepared. They also lit a huge bonfire to honor him.
\c 17
\p
\v 1 Asa’s son Jehoshaphat became the next king of Judah. He built a strong army to defend Judah against attacks from the army of Israel.
\v 2 He stationed soldiers in all the cities in Judah that had walls around them. He also stationed soldiers to guard other places that were important for defending Judah. He also stationed soldiers in the cities in the territory of Ephraim that his father Asa had captured.
\p
\v 3 Yahweh helped Jehoshaphat because he lived as his father had lived in the first part of his reign. Jehoshaphat did not worship Baal.
\v 4 Instead, he worshiped the God whom his father had worshiped. He obeyed God’s commands, and he did not do the evil things that the kings of Israel did.
\v 5 Yahweh enabled him to become completely in control of his kingdom. People from throughout Judah brought gifts to Jehoshaphat. As a result, he became very rich, and people respected him greatly.
\v 6 He became bold to do what would please Yahweh. He even commanded his workers to destroy the hilltop shrines where people had been worshiping idols. He also commanded them to cut down the wooden images throughout Judah that represented the goddess Asherah.
\p
\v 7 After Jehoshaphat had been ruling for almost three years, he sent some of his officials to teach the people who lived in various cities in Judah. Those officials were Ben Hail, Obadiah, Zechariah, Nethanel, and Micaiah.
\v 8 He sent Levites and priests to help them. The Levites were Shemaiah, Nethaniah, Zebadiah, Asahel, Shemiramoth, Jehonathan, Adonijah, Tobijah, and Tob Adonijah. The priests were Elishama and Jehoram.
\v 9 So that they could teach the Judeans, they brought with them a scroll that recorded Yahweh’s law. They went to cities throughout Judah and taught the people who lived in those places.
\p
\v 10 All the kings who ruled the countries in the area around Judah became very afraid of Yahweh. As a result, they did not attack Jehoshaphat.
\v 11 Some of the Philistine rulers sent gifts to Jehoshaphat, and they sent silver to him as tribute. The rulers of the nearby Arabian tribes also sent him large numbers of rams and goats that demonstrated their complete submission to him.
\p
\v 12 Jehoshaphat continued to become a more powerful king. He had his workers build forts and places to store supplies in various locations in Judah.
\v 13 He did many things to fortify the cities of Judah. He also stationed in Jerusalem many capable soldiers who could fight well.
\v 14 Soldiers from the same clan fought together. This is how many soldiers were in Jehoshaphat’s army and who their commanders were.
\q1 Adnah commanded the entire army of Judah, and he directly commanded 300,000 capable soldiers.
\q1 \v 15 Jehohanan was the next highest-ranking officer. He commanded 280,000 other capable soldiers.
\q1 \v 16 Amasiah son of Zichri was the next highest-ranking officer. He volunteered to serve Yahweh in that way. He commanded 200,000 other capable soldiers.
\q1 \v 17 Eliada, who was a great soldier, commanded the army of Benjamin. He directly commanded 200,000 soldiers who used bows and arrows and shields.
\q1 \v 18 The next highest-ranking officer was Jehozabad. He commanded 180,000 men who had weapons for fighting battles.
\m
\v 19 Jehoshaphat had all of these soldiers available to fight for him, as well as the ones he had stationed in various cities in Judah that had walls around them.
\c 18
\p
\v 1 Jehoshaphat had become very rich, and people respected him greatly. But then, to make an alliance with King Ahab of Israel, he arranged for his son Jehoram to marry Ahab’s daughter Athaliah.
\v 2 A few years later, Jehoshaphat went to visit Ahab at his palace in Samaria. Ahab slaughtered many sheep and cattle to make a feast for him and the officials he had brought with him. {At the feast,} Ahab tried to persuade him to help him recapture Ramoth Gilead.
\v 3 Finally Ahab asked Jehoshaphat directly, “Will you lead your Judean army and join me as I lead my Israelite army to fight to recapture Ramoth Gilead?” Jehoshaphat replied, “Yes, I will lead my army and join you as you lead your army. My soldiers will fight alongside your soldiers.”
\v 4 Then Jehoshaphat added, “But we should first ask Yahweh whether we should attack Ramoth Gilead.”
\p
\v 5 So Ahab summoned his prophets. There were 400 of them. He asked them, “Should I lead my army to fight to recapture Ramoth Gilead, or should I not do that?”
\p They answered, “Yes, go and attack that city, because God will enable your army to recapture it.”
\p
\v 6 But Jehoshaphat asked, “Is there still a prophet of Yahweh here whom we could ask about this?”
\p
\v 7 King Ahab replied, “There is still one prophet whom we can ask to tell us what Yahweh says. His name is Micaiah son of Imlah. But I hate him, because whenever he prophesies about me, he never says that anything good will happen to me. Instead, he always predicts that bad things will happen to me.”
\p Jehoshaphat replied, “I do not feel that you should assume he will do that.”
\p
\v 8 So King Ahab told one of his officers to summon Micaiah immediately.
\p
\v 9 Ahab, the king of Israel, and Jehoshaphat, the king of Judah, were both wearing their royal robes. They were each sitting on thrones in an open square just inside one of the gates in the wall of Samaria. Many prophets were standing in front of them and speaking messages that they said were from God.
\p
\v 10 One of those prophets was Zedekiah son of Kenaanah. He used iron to make some horns, then he put them on his head and acted like a bull that was goring another animal. He proclaimed to Ahab, “Yahweh. says that your army will keep defeating the Arameans until you have completely destroyed them, just as a bull keeps goring another animal until it has killed it!”
\v 11 The other prophets who were there said the same thing. They told Ahab, “If you go and attack Ramoth Gilead, you will succeed in conquering it, because Yahweh will enable you to defeat its defenders!”
\p
\v 12 Meanwhile, the messenger who went to summon Micaiah told him, “Listen to me! All the other prophets are predicting that King Ahab will defeat the Arameans with his army. So be sure that you say the same thing and also predict that he will defeat them.”
\p
\v 13 But Micaiah replied, “I swear by Yahweh that I will tell Ahab exactly what my God tells me to say.”
\p
\v 14 When Micaiah came to Ahab, Ahab asked him, “Micaiah, should I lead my army to fight to recapture Ramoth Gilead, or should I not do that?”
\p Micaiah replied, “If you go and attack Ramoth Gilead, you will succeed in conquering it, because Yahweh will enable you to defeat its defenders.”
\p
\v 15 But King Ahab {realized that Micaiah did not really mean what he was saying, so he} told Micaiah, “I have told you many times that you must always speak truthfully when you say what Yahweh has revealed to you about me!”
\p
\v 16 So Micaiah said to him, “{The truth is that in a vision} I saw all the Israelite soldiers had scattered on the hills, the way sheep scatter when they do not have a shepherd. And Yahweh said, ‘Their master is dead. So let them all stop fighting and go home.’”
\p
\v 17 Then Ahab said to Jehoshaphat, “I told you that he never says that anything good will happen to me. Instead, he always predicts that bad things will happen to me!”
\p
\v 18 Then Micaiah continued, “Let me tell you what Yahweh showed to me! {In a vision} I saw Yahweh sitting on his throne. The angels of heaven were standing on both sides of him.
\v 19 And Yahweh asked, ‘Who can persuade Ahab to lead his army to attack Ramoth Gilead so that he will die in battle there?’
\p Some said to do that in one way, while others said to do it in a different way.
\p
\v 20 Finally a spirit came forward and stood in front of Yahweh. It told him, ‘I will deceive him!’
\p Yahweh asked him, ‘How will you do that?’
\p
\v 21 The spirit replied, ‘I will go and inspire all of Ahab’s prophets to tell lies.’
\p Yahweh said, ‘You will be successful in getting them to tell lies. Go and do that!’
\p
\v 22 So I am telling you that Yahweh has caused all of your prophets to lie to you. Yahweh has {done that because he has} decided that something terrible is going to happen to you.”
\p
\v 23 Then Zedekiah walked over to Micaiah and slapped him on his face. He said, “Yahweh’s Spirit has not left me in order to speak to you! That has not really happened!”
\p
\v 24 Micaiah replied, “You will find out for yourself {to which of us Yahweh’s Spirit has truly spoken} on the day when you hide in the back room of a house!”
\p
\v 25 King Ahab commanded his soldiers, “Seize Micaiah and take him to Amon, the governor of this city, and to my son Joash.
\v 26 Tell them that I am commanding them to put this man in prison and give him only a little food to eat and a little water to drink each day until I return safely from the battle.”
\p
\v 27 Micaiah replied, “If you actually return safely, it will be clear that I was not speaking a message from Yahweh” Then he said to all those who were standing there, “All of you, pay attention to what I have told King Ahab!”
\p
\v 28 So King Ahab of Israel and King Jehoshaphat of Judah led their armies to attack Ramoth Gilead.
\v 29 King Ahab said to Jehoshaphat, “When I lead my army into battle, I will wear different clothes {so that no one will recognize that I am the king of Israel}. But you should wear your royal robes.” So Ahab put on different clothes, and they both led their armies into the battle.
\p
\v 30 The king of Aram had told the men who commanded his chariot forces, “Find the king of Israel and pursue and kill only him. Do not pursue anyone else.”
\v 31 So when the commanders of the Aramean chariot forces saw Jehoshaphat {wearing his royal robes}, they shouted, “There is the king of Israel!” They started chasing him to attack him. But Jehoshaphat prayed out loud, and Yahweh protected him. He made the commanders stop chasing him.
\v 32 Those commanders realized that Jehoshaphat was not the king of Israel. So they stopped pursuing him.
\p
\v 33 But one Aramean soldier shot an arrow at the Israelite soldiers without aiming at anyone in particular. The arrow struck Ahab where the parts of his armor joined together {and so it went through the armor and into his body}. Ahab told the driver of his chariot, “This arrow has wounded me! So turn the chariot around and get me out of the midst of the fighting!”
\v 34 The Israelites and Arameans fought desperately throughout that day. Ahab’s soldiers helped him keep standing up all day in his chariot facing the Aramean troops. But just as the sun was setting, he died.
\c 19
\p
\v 1 But King Jehoshaphat was able to return safely to his palace in Jerusalem.
\v 2 The prophet Jehu son of Hanani came out of the city to meet him {as he was returning}. He told him, “It was not right for you to help a wicked man or to befriend someone who hated Yahweh. Yahweh is angry with you for doing that.
\v 3 However, you have also done some good things. After all, you have destroyed the wooden images in your kingdom that represented the goddess Asherah. You have determined that you will worship only the true God.”
\p
\v 4 Jehoshaphat lived in Jerusalem. But he left the city again in order to speak with people throughout his kingdom, from the city of Beersheba {in the south} to the hilly area {in the north} where the descendants of Ephraim lived. He got them to worship Yahweh again, the God whom their ancestors had worshiped.
\v 5 He appointed judges in each of the cities throughout the kingdom of Judah that had walls around them. He made sure that each city had its own judges.
\v 6 He told them, “You will not be judging cases on behalf of people, but on behalf of Yahweh. So make your decisions carefully. Yahweh will help you make the right decisions.
\v 7 When you judge on behalf of Yahweh our God, you must never acquit guilty people or condemn innocent people. You must never treat some people more favorably than others. You must never take a bribe. So make sure that you revere Yahweh and judge cases honestly.”
\p
\v 8 After Jehoshaphat returned to Jerusalem, he also appointed some Levites, priests, and leaders of Israelite families to judge cases there according to Yahweh’s laws.
\v 9 He ordered those judges, “You must revere Yahweh and judge cases honestly and completely sincerely.
\v 10 Your fellow Israelites who live in other cities will bring you cases that are too difficult for their judges to settle. Some cases will be about whether a person has murdered someone or killed him accidentally. Other cases will deal with various other regulations in the law of Moses. You must warn those Israelites that they must tell the truth throughout the whole trial. If they did not, they would sin against Yahweh, and he would become angry with you and with them. So warn them, and then none of you will become guilty.
\p
\v 11 I want you to know that Amariah, the high priest, will help you with any matter that relates to the worship of Yahweh and to his laws. Zebadiah son of Ishmael, the leader of the tribe of Judah, will help you with any matter that relates to me and my kingdom. Levites will serve as your court officers. So act courageously! Yahweh will help you if you want to do your work honestly.”
\c 20
\p
\v 1 But after he had done these good things, a large army marched to attack Jehoshaphat. Its soldiers were Moabites, Ammonites, and Meunites.
\p
\v 2 People came and told Jehoshaphat, “A huge army is coming to attack you! They are coming from the region of Edom, on the other side of the Sea of Salt. They have already gotten as far as Hazezon Tamar!” (Another name for that place is Engedi.)
\v 3 This made Jehoshaphat very afraid. He determined to ask Yahweh what he should do. He commanded everyone in Judah to eat little food {to show how much distress they felt}.
\v 4 People from Judah gathered in Jerusalem to ask Yahweh to help them. They came from cities throughout Judah to do that.
\p
\v 5 People from Jerusalem and throughout Judah gathered in the large public courtyard of the temple. Jehoshaphat stood in front of them
\v 6 and he prayed, “Yahweh, you are the God whom our ancestors worshiped. You are the God who reigns from heaven. You rule over all the different kingdoms on earth. You possess such great power that no one can successfully oppose you.
\v 7 You, our God, are the one who forced the people who lived in this land to leave so that your Israelite people could live here. {Our ancestor} Abraham was your friend, and you gave this land to him and his descendants forever.
\v 8 We Israelites have been living here, and we have built a temple for you here where we worship you. When King Solomon dedicated the temple, he prayed,
\v 9 ‘We may experience disasters because you are punishing us. Enemies may attack us, or diseases may spread, or we may not be able to grow enough food. If that happens, since you have promised to be present here, we will assemble at this temple where you are present. We will plead with you to help us, and may you do what we ask and rescue us.’
\v 10 Here is the problem we now have. You did not allow our Israelite ancestors to enter the countries of Ammon, Moab, or Edom when they were traveling from Egypt to Canaan. So our ancestors went around those areas, and they did not destroy the people who lived there.
\v 11 But now they are doing something bad to us, even though we were kind to them. They are trying to force us to leave the land that you gave to us as a place that would always belong to us!
\v 12 Our God, please decide that they are wrong and defeat them. We ask you to do that because we are not able to defeat this huge army that is attacking us. We do not know what to do, but we are depending on you to help us.”
\p
\v 13 All the men of Judah were standing there with their wives, children, and babies in the presence of Yahweh {while Jehoshaphat prayed}.
\p
\v 14 In that great crowd there was a man whose name was Jahaziel. He was the son of Zechariah, the grandson of Benaiah, the great-grandson of Jeiel, and the great-great-grandson of Mattaniah. He was a Levite who was a descendant of Asaph. Yahweh’s Spirit inspired him,
\v 15 and he said, “King Jehoshaphat and you people who live in Jerusalem and in other places throughout Judah, listen to me! This is what Yahweh says to you: ‘You will not have to fight against this huge army, because God will fight them and defeat them. So do not be afraid of them, and do not lose courage.
\v 16 Tomorrow, march toward them. I can tell you that they will be coming up through the Ascent of Ziz. You will meet them at the end of the ravine near the wilderness of Jeruel.
\v 17 But you will not need to fight a battle against them. The soldiers from Jerusalem and other places in Judah may form a battle line, but they should then stand still and watch what Yahweh does to save you. Do not be afraid and do not lose courage. March toward them tomorrow, and Yahweh will help you.’”
\p
\v 18 {When he heard this,} Jehoshaphat bowed down with his face touching the ground. All the people who were there from Jerusalem and other places in Judah bowed down in Yahweh’s presence to worship him.
\v 19 Then some Levites who were descendants of Kohath and Korah stood up and very loudly praised Yahweh, the God of the Israelites.
\p
\v 20 Early the next morning, the army left to go to the desolate area near the town of Tekoa. As they were leaving, Jehoshaphat stood up and said to them, “You people from Jerusalem and other places in Judah, listen to me! Trust Yahweh our God! If you do that, you will be strong. Believe what he inspires people to say! If you do that, you will be successful.”
\v 21 After discussing this with his military officers, Jehoshaphat had Levites march ahead of the soldiers. Those were Levites whose ministry was to sing to Yahweh and praise him for how wonderfully holy he is. They were singing,
\q1 “Yahweh loves us faithfully forever,
\q2 so praise him!”
\p
\v 22 The Ammonite, Moabite, and Edomite soldiers had been coming to attack Judah. But as soon as the Levites started to shout and praise Yahweh, Yahweh caused those soldiers to attack and kill each other.
\v 23 What happened was that the Ammonite and Moabite soldiers first attacked the Edomite soldiers and completely destroyed them. After they had killed all of them, the Ammonitte and Moabite soldiers all killed each other.
\p
\v 24 When the Judeans came to the place where they could look out over the desolate area, they saw where that huge army was. But they saw that its soldiers lying dead on the ground! Not one of them was still alive.
\p
\v 25 So Jehoshaphat and his soldiers went to take the valuable possessions that had belonged to their enemies. They found much equipment and clothing and other valuable items. They collected these things until they could not carry any more. There were so many things that it took them three days to collect all of them.
\v 26 The next day, they gathered in the Valley of Beracah. They praised Yahweh there, and that is why people called that place the Valley of Beracah{, which means “praise.”} People still call it that today.
\p
\v 27 Then Jehoshaphat led all the soldiers from Jerusalem and other places in Judah back home. Yahweh had enabled them to defeat their enemies, so they were very happy as they returned to Jerusalem.
\v 28 When they arrived in Jerusalem, they went to the temple {to praise and thank Yahweh}. Levites and priests were playing lyres, harps, and trumpets.
\p
\v 29 The people of the kingdoms around Judah became very afraid of God when they learned how Yahweh had defeated the enemies of the Israelites.
\v 30 After that, God kept the countries around him from attacking him, so Jehoshaphat’s kingdom was a peaceful place.
\p
\v 31 Jehoshaphat continued to rule Judah. He had been 35 years old when he started to rule, and he ruled in Jerusalem for 25 years. His mother was a woman whose name was Azubah. She was the daughter of Shilhi.
\v 32 Throughout his life, Jehoshaphat lived as Yahweh wanted people to live, just as his father Asa had done.
\v 33 However, he did not order his servants to destroy the shrines for worship on the top of hills. Most of the people were still not serious about obeying the God whom their ancestors had worshiped.
\p
\v 34 The accounts that Jehu son of Hanani wrote describe further things that Jehoshaphat did throughout his reign. Those accounts are in the book in which the kings of Israel recorded what happened during their reigns.
\p
\v 35 Later, Jehoshaphat, the king of Judah, made a peace treaty with Ahaziah, the king of Israel. That was a bad thing for Jehoshaphat to do.
\v 36 They agreed to have their workers build a fleet of ships that were able to sail out on the sea. They built those ships at Ezion Geber.
\v 37 But Eliezer son of Dodavahu from the city of Mareshah brought a message from God to Jehoshaphat. He told him, “Because you made a treaty with {that wicked king} Ahaziah, Yahweh is going to destroy what you have built.” Then there was a great storm at Ezion Geber that wrecked the ships, so they were never able to sail anywhere.
\c 21
\p
\v 1 Then Jehoshaphat died. The Judeans buried him where they had buried his ancestors, in the part of Jerusalem that people called the City of David. His son Jehoram became the next king of Judah.
\v 2 The Israelite king Jehoshaphat had several other sons who were Jehoram’s younger brothers. They were Azariah, Jehiel, Zechariah, Azariahu, Michael, and Shephatiah.
\v 3 Their father had given them generous gifts of silver and gold and other valuable things. He also appointed them to rule various cities in Judah that had walls around them. But since Jehoram was his oldest son, he appointed him to be the king of Judah.
\p
\v 4 After Jehoram had become king and was completely in control of his father’s kingdom, he had his soldiers execute all of his younger brothers. He also had them execute some of the leaders of the nation.
\v 5 Jehoram was 32 years old when he became the king, and he ruled in Jerusalem for 8 years.
\v 6 Because his wife was one of Ahab’s daughters, he did the same kind of evil things that the kings of Israel did who were from Ahab’s family. He did things that Yahweh considered to be evil.
\v 7 However, because Yahweh had made a covenant with King David, he did want to not end his dynasty. After all, Yahweh had promised David that his descendants would always be kings.
\p
\v 8 Judah had controlled Edom, but during Jehoram’s reign, the Edomites rebelled. They appointed their own king.
\v 9 So Jehoram went with his army commanders and all of his chariots {to try to reconquer Edom}. But the Edomite commanders surrounded them with their chariots. Jehoram had to make a surprise attack against them at night (in order to escape}.
\v 10 Since that time, Judah has no longer controlled Edom. Then, around that same time, the people of the city of Libnah also rebelled against Judah. Those things happened because Jehoram stopped obeying Yahweh, the God whom his ancestors had worshiped.
\p
\v 11 Instead, he built hilltop shrines for worshiping idols throughout Judah. He led the people who lived in Jerusalem and in other places in Judah be unfaithful to Yahweh by worshiping other gods.
\p
\v 12 So the prophet Elijah sent a letter to him. In it, he said, “Yahweh is the God whom your ancestor King David worshiped. This is what he says to you: ‘Bad things are going to happen to you because you have not lived as the previous kings of Judah lived. They were your father Jehoshaphat and {your grandfather} Asa.
\v 13 Instead, you have lived as the kings of Israel live. You have led the people who live in Jerusalem and other places in Judah to be unfaithful to Yahweh by worshiping other gods. That is what the kings from the family of Ahab have done. You have also killed your brothers. They were part of the royal family, and they were better men than you are.
\v 14 As a result, I, Yahweh, am about to cause great harm to the people of your kingdom, including your wives and children. I will also destroy much of your property.
\v 15 I am also going to make you very sick. You will have a disease in your intestines that will become steadily worse until it makes your intestines come out.’”
\p
\v 16 Then Yahweh influenced some of the Philistines and some of the Arabs who lived near the Cushites to attack Jehoram.
\v 17 They invaded Judah and defeated its defenders. That enabled them to go to Jerusalem and take away many valuable things that they found in the king’s palace. They also took away Jehoshaphat’s sons and wives. His youngest son, Jehoahaz, was the only one of his sons whom they did not take away.
\p
\v 18 After those bad things happened, Yahweh caused Jehoram to have an intestinal disease that no one could cure.
\v 19 As a result, he was in severe pain every day. This continued for two whole years. At the end of that time, the disease made his intestines come out, and he died. The people of Judah had made great bonfires to honor his ancestors when they died. But they did not make a bonfire for Jehoram.
\p
\v 20 Jehoram was 32 years old when he became the king, and he ruled in Jerusalem for 8 years. When he died, no one wished that he were still alive. The Judeans buried him in the part of Jerusalem that people called the city of David. But they did not bury him in the royal tombs.
\c 22
\p
\v 1 The Philistines and Arabs who had invaded Judah had killed all of Jehoram’s older sons. So the people who lived in Jerusalem appointed his youngest son Ahaziah to be their next king. Then Ahaziah ruled Judah after his father King Jehoram.
\p
\v 2 Ahaziah was 42 years old when he became the king. He ruled in Jerusalem for one year. His mother was a woman whose name was Athaliah. She was the granddaughter of King Omri of Israel.
\p
\v 3 Ahaziah’s mother advised him what to do, but she told him to do bad things. So he behaved just as the kings of Israel did who were descendants of Ahab.
\v 4 Ahab’s family also advised him what to do after his father died. As a result, he did many things that Yahweh had told the Israelites were wrong. Ultimately, this destroyed him.
\v 5 King Jehoram of Israel, who was Ahab’s son, led his soldiers to fight against the army of King Hazael of Aram. Following the advice of Ahab’s family, Ahaziah led his soldiers to help Jehoram. The armies fought near the city of Ramoth Gilead. The Aramean soldiers wounded Jehoram in the battle there.
\p
\v 6 Because the Arameans had wounded him when he fought with Hazael their king, Jehoram went back to the city of Jezreel to recover from his wounds. King Ahaziah of Judah learned that he had gone there to recover. So he went to Jezreel to visit him.
\p
\v 7 God influenced Ahaziah to go to visit Joram so that he would die at Jezreel. When Ahaziah arrived there, he went with Jehoram to meet Jehu son of Nimshi. Yahweh had appointed him to kill the descendants of Ahab.
\v 8 While Jehu and the men who were with him were killing Ahab’s descendants, they found the leaders of Judah and the sons of Ahaziah’s relatives who were officers in Ahaziah’s royal court. They killed them too.
\v 9 Then Jehu ordered his soldiers to look for Ahaziah. They found Ahaziah hiding in the city of Samaria. They brought him to Jehu and executed him. But they felt that they ought to bury him because he was a descendant of Jehoshaphat, who tried hard to please Yahweh. So they did bury him. After that, there were no descendants of Ahaziah who were powerful enough to become the next king of Judah.
\p
\v 10 Instead, when Ahaziah’s mother Athaliah learned that Jehu had killed her son, she ordered soldiers to kill all the members of Ahaziah’s family who might become the next king.
\v 11 But King Jehoram had a daughter whose name was Jehoshabeath. She was the half-sister of Ahaziah, and she was the wife of Jehoiada the high priest. She rescued Ahaziah’s very young son Joash. While soldiers were killing his other sons, she took him away and hid him and his nursemaid in a room in the palace where people kept bedding supplies. Because she hid him, Athaliah did not know where he was, so her soldiers did not kill him.
\v 12 For the next six years, the priests hid Joash in God’s temple. He lived with Jehoiada and Jehoshabeath. Meanwhile, Athaliah ruled Judah.
\c 23
\p
\v 1 But after those six years, Jehoiada courageously made an agreement {to make Josiah the king} with some officers who each commanded a hundred soldiers. They were Azariah son of Jeroham, Ishmael son of Jehohanan, Azariah son of Obed, Maaseiah son of Adaiah, and Elishaphat son of Zichri.
\v 2 Those men went throughout Judah to get Levites who lived in various cities in Judah along with Israelite clan leaders. They brought them back to Jerusalem.
\v 3 They gathered at God’s temple. Jehoiada told them, “Yahweh said that the descendants of David should be our kings. So his descendant Josiah should be our king.” The Levites and leaders made a solemn agreement to obey Josiah as their king.
\p
\v 4 Jehoiada then gave them these instructions: “Many priests and Levites will come on duty on the Sabbath day. When they do{, the priests and Levites who have been here at the temple should not leave.} Instead, divide yourselves into three groups. One group must guard the entrances to the temple area.
\v 5 Another group must watch the royal palace. The last group must guard the gate that leads from the palace to the temple. All the other people who have come here will stay in the courtyards in front of the temple.
\v 6 Do not allow anyone to enter the temple except the priests and the Levites who are serving there. Yahweh has set them apart for their work, so they may go inside. But everyone else must obey Yahweh’s command not to enter the temple.
\v 7 The Levites are to arm themselves with their weapons and stand all around the king to protect him. They are to kill anyone who tries to get into the temple {to harm the king}. If the king goes anywhere, they are to go with him.”
\p
\v 8 The Levites and all the Judeans who had come to Jerusalem did exactly what Jehoiada the priest told them to do. Jehoiada had told the Levites who were going off duty to stay at the temple, so they did. So the Levite leaders each organized those who were about to start their work on the Sabbath day and those who were just finishing their work.
\v 9 Some spears, shields and large shields that had belonged to King David were in the temple. Jehoiada distributed them to the officers who commanded a hundred soldiers {to give to their men}.
\v 10 Jehoiada had the soldiers with those weapons form a line all across the front of the temple. They stood between the temple building and the altar in its courtyard. In that way, they provided complete protection for the king.
\p
\v 11 Then Jehoiada brought Joash, the son of the last king, out of the temple. He put a crown on his head, and he gave him a scroll that recorded Yahweh’s laws. Then he and his sons poured olive oil on Joash’s head {to show that he was now the king}. The people shouted, “May King Joash rule for a long time!”
\p
\v 12 Athaliah heard the noise that the people were making as they ran over to Joash and as they shouted to praise him. So she came to the temple where the people had gathered.
\v 13 She saw Joash, the new king, standing next to one of the big pillars on the temple porch. She saw military commanders and musicians playing trumpets standing next to him. She heard a crowd of ordinary Israelites shouting joyfully. The priests were blowing trumpets, and the Levites were singing and playing musical instruments to lead the people in worship. She tore her clothes {to show her distress} and shouted, “You are traitors! You have betrayed me!”
\p
\v 14 Jehoiada told the officers who commanded a hundred soldiers, whom he had put in charge of the armed men that day, “Have the guards take her away from the temple. Kill anyone who tries to rescue her!” He said that because he did not want his men to kill her near Yahweh’s temple.
\v 15 The guards formed two lines so that she could walk safely between them {back to the palace}. She walked through the entrance that horses used to get into the palace courtyard. The guards killed her there.
\v 16 Then Jehoiada had the people make a solemn agreement with him and with the king that they would always be loyal to Yahweh as their God.
\v 17 Then the large crowd of Israelites who had gathered went to the temple of Baal. They destroyed the main altar, smashed the statues of Baal into pieces, and tore down the temple building. They also killed Mattan, the priest of Baal, right in front of the other altars in the temple.
\p
\v 18 Then Jehoiada organized the priests to resume their duties at the temple. They were to offer sacrifices to Yahweh that they burned completely on the altar, as Moses had said in the law that they should do. He also organized the Levites to resume their duties. David had said that they should perform joyful music at the temple, and he had assigned various responsibilities to different groups of Levites.
\v 19 Jehoiada also had the Levite gatekeepers resume their duties of guarding the gates of the temple courtyard. They were to make sure that only people who were ritually clean entered the courtyard.
\p
\v 20 Then Jehoiada gathered the officers who commanded a hundred soldiers, the Judean nobles, other Judean leaders, and all the people who had come from throughout Judea. They brought the king from the temple to the palace. Joash entered the palace through its Upper Gate. The people had him sit down on the same throne where the previous kings of Judah had sat.
\v 21 People throughout Judah were glad that the guards had executed Athaliah. The city of Jerusalem became a peaceful place after she died.
\c 24
\p
\v 1 Joash was seven years old when he became the king of Judah, and he ruled in Jerusalem for 40 years. His mother was a woman whose name was Zibiah. She was from the city of Beersheba.
\v 2 Joash did what Yahweh considered to be right for as long as Jehoiada was the high priest.
\v 3 Jehoiada arranged for Joash to marry two wives. He had sons and daughters with them.
\p
\v 4 Later, Joash wanted to repair the temple.
\v 5 He summoned the priests and Levites and told them, “Go throughout Judah and collect from the people the money that the law of Moses requires them to pay each year. Then use that money to pay for repairing the temple. Do that immediately.” But the Levites did not do that immediately.
\p
\v 6 So the king summoned Jehoiada, the high priest. He told him, “Yahweh’s servant Moses required the Israelites to pay a tax to care for the sacred tent. You should have required the Levites to collect that tax from the people who live in Jerusalem and other places in Judah {so that we could repair the temple}.”
\p
\v 7 The temple needed repairs because the sons of that wicked woman Athaliah had broken into it. They had also taken the special items that were in it and used them to worship Baal.
\p
\v 8 King Joash commanded Jehoiada to make a chest and put it just outside the entrance to the temple courtyard.
\v 9 Then the king sent messengers to tell the people who lived in Jerusalem and other places in Judah to bring their tax money to the temple. That is what God’s servant Moses had required the Israelites to do when they were in the desolate area between Egypt and Canaan.
\v 10 All of the Judean leaders and other people agreed, and they gladly brought their contributions. They put their money into the chest and filled it up.
\v 11 From time to time, the Levites would see that there was a lot of money in the chest. When that happened, they would carry the chest to the officials whom the king had put in charge of the temple restoration. The king’s secretary and the assistant to the high priest would come and take the money out of the chest. Then the Levites would carry the chest back and put it outside the courtyard entrance again. They did this regularly, and they collected a great amount of money.
\v 12 The king and Jehoiada paid the money to the men who were supervising the work of repairing the temple. Those men hired stoneworkers and carpenters to repair the temple. They also hired metalworkers to make items of iron and bronze to restore the temple.
\p
\v 13 The men who were making the repairs worked hard, and through their efforts they put the temple in good condition again. They restored the temple according to its original design, and they made it stronger.
\v 14 There was still money left when they had finished the repair work. So they brought that money to the king and to Jehoiada. They told the workers to use that money to make items for the priests to use in the temple. So they made tools for the priests to use when offering sacrifices that that they burned completely on the altar. They also made dishes and other gold and silver items for the temple. For long as Jehoiada lived, the priests offered sacrifices at the temple each morning and evening that they burned completely on the altar.
\p
\v 15 Jehoiada lived for a long time. He was 130 years old when he died.
\v 16 The Judeans buried him in the part of Jerusalem that people called the City of David. That is where they buried their kings. They buried him there because he had done good things for the Israelites and for God and his temple.
\p
\v 17 But after Jehoiada died, the Judean leaders came to Joash and bowed down low in front of him {to flatter him}. Then they persuaded him to do what they wanted.
\v 18 The Judeans stopped worshiping Yahweh, the God whom their ancestors had worshiped, at his temple. Instead, they worshiped Asherah and other false gods. Yahweh became very angry with the people who lived in Jerusalem and other places in Judah for sinning in this way.
\v 19 Yahweh sent prophets to tell them to worship him again. The prophets showed the people that they were wrong to worship other gods. But the people did not do what the prophets told them to do.
\p
\v 20 Then God’s Spirit inspired Zechariah, who was the son of Jehoiada the high priest. He stood up {n the inner courtyard of the temple} above the people {who were in the outer courtyard}. He told them, “This is what God says, ‘You must not disobey the commandments that I, Yahweh, have given you! You will not succeed if you do that. Because you no longer worship me, Yahweh, I will no longer protect you.”
\p
\v 21 But the leaders of Judah made a secret plan against Zechariah. King Joash ordered them to kill him. So they killed him by throwing stones at him in the temple courtyard.
\v 22 Zechariah’s father Jehoiada had always helped King Joash, but he was not grateful for that. Instead, he commanded people to kill Jehoiada’s son. That son, Zechariah, said as he was dying, “I hope that Yahweh will recognize what a wicked thing these people have done and punish them for doing it!”
\p
\v 23 In the spring of the following year, the army of Aram attacked Joash’s kingdom. They invaded Judah and conquered Jerusalem. They killed all of the Judean leaders. They took many valuable possessions and sent them back to their king, who ruled in the city of Damascus.
\v 24 The Aramean army that invaded Judah did not have very many soldiers. The Judean army was much larger. But Yahweh allowed the Arameans to defeat the Judeans. Yahweh did that to punish Joash because he had led the Judeans to stop worshiping him, the God whom their ancestors had worshiped.
\v 25 The Arameans severely wounded Joash when he fought against them. So after the Arameans left, some of his officials made a secret plan against him because he had killed Zechariah and the other sons of Jehoiada the high priest. They killed him while he was lying in bed trying to recover from his wounds. When Joash died, the Judeans buried him in the part of Jerusalem that people called the City of David. But they did not bury him in the royal tombs.
\p
\v 26 One of the officials who made a secret plan against Joash was Zabad. His mother Shimeath was an Ammonitess. The other official was Jehozabad. His mother Shimrith was a Moabitess.
\v 27 There is an account of Joash’s reign in the book in which the kings of Judah and Israel recorded what happened during their reigns. This account describes Joash’s sons and what they did. It also records the many oracles that prophets spoke against him. It also describes what he did to repair the temple. After Joash died, his son Amaziah became the next king.
\c 25
\p
\v 1 Amaziah was 25 years old when he became the king of Judah, and he ruled in Jerusalem for 29 years. His mother’s name was Jehoaddan. She was from Jerusalem.
\v 2 Amaziah did what Yahweh considered to be right, but not wholeheartedly.
\v 3 As soon as he completely controlled his kingdom, he had his soldiers execute the officials who had murdered his father, King Joash.
\v 4 But he did not order his soldiers to execute their sons. He obeyed what Moses wrote in the book of the law. Yahweh had commanded, “You must not execute parents for what their children do, and you must not execute children for what their parents do. Execute people only for the sins that they themselves commit.”
\p
\v 5 Amaziah ordered the men in his kingdom to come to Jerusalem. He organized the men of the tribes of Judah and Benjamin by clans into military units. He appointed officers to command groups of a thousand soldiers and of a hundred soldiers. They counted the men who were at least 20 years old, and they found that there were 300,000 men who were capable soldiers who could fight well with spears and shields.
\v 6 Amaziah also hired 100,000 capable soldiers from Israel. He paid almost 3,300 kilograms of silver to hire them.
\p
\v 7 But a prophet came to him and told him, “Your Majesty, Yahweh does not help any soldiers from the northern kingdom of Israel. So you must not allow those soldiers from Israel to march with your soldiers.
\v 8 God is able to help an army win a battle or to make an army lose a battle. So you and your own soldiers must be courageous and go and fight. {If you rely on soldiers from Israel,} God will allow your enemies to defeat you.
\p
\v 9 Amaziah told that prophet, “If those soldiers do not help us, then I will get nothing for the 3,300 kilograms of silver that I paid to hire them!”
\p The prophet replied, “Yahweh is able to give you much more money than you paid to hire those soldiers.”
\p
\v 10 So Amaziah removed the Israelite soldiers from his army and told them to go back home. This made them very angry with the Judeans. As they started to march back home, they were still very angry.
\p
\v 11 Amaziah felt brave enough to lead an army of only his own soldiers. They fought against the Edomites in the Valley of Salt and killed 10,000 of them.
\v 12 The Judeans also captured 10,000 other soldiers. They took them to the top of a cliff and threw them down over the cliff. Their bodies broke apart when they hit the ground{, and they all died}.
\p
\v 13 Meanwhile, the soldiers whom Amaziah had hired but then not allowed to fight with him attacked cities in Judah. They had come from the city of Samaria {in Israel}, and they attacked cities as far into Judah {from there} as Beth Horon. They killed 3,000 people and took away many valuable things.
\p
\v 14 Amaziah returned to Jerusalem after his army had defeated the Edomites. He had captured some idols that represented the gods that people worshiped in Edom. He made a shrine for them as his own gods. Then he bowed down to worship them, and he offered sacrifices to them.
\v 15 This made Yahweh very angry with Amaziah. He sent a prophet to him, who told him, “It does not make sense for you to worship these gods! When your army attacked the people who worshiped them, they were not able to protect them!”
\p
\v 16 But while he was still speaking, the king told him, “You are not one of my advisors. So stop talking! Otherwise, I will have my soldiers execute you!”
\p So the prophet stopped delivering this message from Yahweh. But he told Amaziah, “I can tell that God has determined to destroy you, because you are refusing to stop worshiping those idols even though I have warned you.”
\p
\v 17 Then King Amaziah of Judah, after consulting his advisors, sent messengers to King Joash of Israel. Joash was the son of Jehoahaz and the grandson of Jehu. The messengers said on behalf of Amaziah, “I challenge you to a battle!”
\p
\v 18 But in response, King Joash sent messengers to tell King Amaziah a story that taught a lesson. They said, “Once a little thornbush was growing in the mountains in Lebanon. It sent a message to a great cedar tree. It told the cedar that it should allow the son of the thornbush to marry its daughter. But as a wild animal in Lebanon was walking along, it stepped on the thornbush and crushed it.
\v 19 You are thinking, ‘I have defeated the Edomites{, so I must have a powerful army}!’ You have become proud and boastful. But you should stay home. If you cause trouble by fighting against me, I will defeat you and harm you and your kingdom.”
\p
\v 20 But Amaziah did not withdraw his challenge. That happened because God wanted Joash’s army to defeat his army because he was worshiping the gods of Edom.
\v 21 So King Joash of Israel led his army to fight against the army of King Amaziah of Judah. They fought a battle at the city of Beth Shemesh in Judah.
\v 22 The Israelite army defeated the army of Judah, and the Judean soldiers ran away and went home.
\v 23 The soldiers of King Joash of Israel captured King Amaziah of Judah at Beth Shemesh. Amaziah was the son of Joash and the grandson of Ahaziah. King Joash brought Amaziah back to Jerusalem {as a prisoner}. His soldiers tore down the wall around the city from the Ephraim Gate to the Corner Gate. That section of the wall was about 180 meters long.
\v 24 His soldiers seized all the gold and silver and valuable items that were in God’s temple that the descendants of Obed Edom were guarding. They also took the valuable things that Amaziah was keeping in his royal palace. They also took some prisoners to make sure that Amaziah would not attack Israel. Then King Joash returned to Samaria.
\p
\v 25 Later, Joash son of Jehoahaz, the king of Israel, died. Amaziah son of Joash, the king of Judah, lived for 15 years after that.
\v 26 The book in which the kings of Judah and Israel recorded what happened during their reigns describes further things that Amaziah did throughout his reign.
\v 27 As soon as Amaziah started to worship other gods instead of Yahweh, some men in Jerusalem made a secret plan to kill him. {When they tried to do that,} he ran away to the city of Lachish. But those people sent men to follow him to Lachish, and those men killed him there.
\v 28 {To honor him,} his officials brought his body back to Jerusalem in his own chariot. They buried him in the part of Jerusalem that people called the City of David. That was where people had also buried his ancestors.
\c 26
\p
\v 1 Then the Judeans appointed Amaziah’s son Uzziah as the next king. He became king when he was 16 years old.
\v 2 After his father Amaziah died, Uzziah led his army to recapture the city of Eloth. He then fortified that city.
\p
\v 3 Uzziah was 16 years old when he started to rule, and he ruled in Jerusalem for 52 years. His mother’s name was Jecoliah. She was from the city of Jerusalem.
\v 4 He did things that Yahweh considered to be right. He did the same good things that his father Amaziah had done.
\v 5 A man whose name was Zechariah taught Uzziah to honor God. For as long as Zechariah was alive, he obeyed God. While Uzziah obeyed Yahweh, God made him successful.
\p
\v 6 Uzziah led his army to fight against the Philistines. They {conquered} the cities of Gath, Jabneh, and Ashdod and tore down their walls. Then he built forts near Ashdod and in other places in Philistia.
\v 7 God helped Uzziah and his army to defeat the armies of the Philistines, the Arabs who lived in the city of Gur Baal, and the Meunites.
\v 8 The Ammonites sent tribute to Uzziah each year. Uzziah became so powerful that even the Egyptians knew that he was a great king.
\p
\v 9 Uzziah had his workers build defensive towers in Jerusalem at the Corner Gate, the Valley Gate, and the place where the wall bends {defensively}. They built very strong towers.
\v 10 Uzziah owned large herds of cattle. They grazed in the foothills of Judah and in the plains. So Uzziah also had his workers build towers in those areas and dig out many cisterns. Uzziah was very interested in farming, so he had his servants farm the land and care for his vineyards in the hills and in the fertile areas.
\p
\v 11 Uzziah had an army of soldiers who fought well. They were in groups that were ready to go out and fight. Jeiel, the king’s secretary, and Maaseiah, one of the army officers, counted the men and placed them in groups. Hananiah, one of the king’s officials, supervised them.
\v 12 Their clan leaders led these capable soldiers into battle. There were 2,600 of those leaders.
\v 13 The groups that those leaders commanded made up an army of 307,500 soldiers who fought well. It was a very powerful army, ready to fight the king’s enemies.
\v 14 Uzziah supplied the entire army with shields, spears, helmets, armor, bows and arrows, and slings for throwing stones.
\v 15 In Jerusalem, his skilled workers made catapults to put on the towers and on the corners of the walls. Those catapults could hurl large stones and launch huge arrows. God helped Uzziah in special ways {as he did these things} so that he became very powerful. As a result, Uzziah became famous even in places that were far from Judah.
\p
\v 16 But when Uzziah became powerful, he became proud, and he did something that was very wrong. He disobeyed Yahweh his God by going inside his temple to burn incense on the altar where only priests could burn incense.
\v 17 Azariah, the high priest, and 80 other brave priests followed him into the temple.
\v 18 They tried to stop him. They told him, “Uzziah, you do not have the right to burn incense to Yahweh {on this altar}! Only the priests, the descendants of Aaron, whom Yahweh set apart, have the right to do that. You have disobeyed Yahweh our God, and he will disgrace you for doing that! So you must leave the temple immediately!”
\p
\v 19 Uzziah became furious with the priests. He was holding a censer and had been just about to burn some incense. He yelled at the priests, and as he did, leprosy appeared on his forehead, right there in the temple next to the incense altar where the priests had confronted him.
\v 20 Azariah the high priest and the other priests looked at him and saw that he had leprosy on his forehead. They made him leave the temple right away. Uzziah himself hurried to leave the temple when he realized that Yahweh had punished him in this way.
\p
\v 21 King Uzziah remained a leper for the rest of his life. So he had to live in a house away from other people, and he could not enter the courtyard of the temple. His son Jotham was in charge of the royal palace and decided cases that Judeans brought to him.
\p
\v 22 The prophet Isaiah son of Amoz wrote an account of further things that Uzziah did throughout his entire reign.
\v 23 When Uzziah died, because he had been a leper, the Judeans did not bury him in the royal tombs. Instead, they buried him in a nearby cemetery that the kings owned. His son Jotham became the next king of Judah.
\c 27
\p
\v 1 Jotham was 25 years old when he became the king of Judah. He ruled from Jerusalem for 16 years. His mother’s name was Jerushah. She was the daughter of Zadok.
\v 2 Jotham did things that Yahweh considered to be right. He did the same things that his father Uzziah had done, except that he did not go into Yahweh’s temple {to burn incense as his father had tried to do}. Nevertheless, the people of Judah continued to worship idols.
\p
\v 3 Jotham had his workers rebuild the Upper Gate of the temple. They also did much to repair the wall on the hill of Ophel.
\v 4 He also had them build cities in the hills of Judah and forts and towers in the forests.
\p
\v 5 He led his army to fight against the Ammonite king and his army, and his soldiers defeated them. He made them to pay a tribute of about three and one-third metric tons of silver, 2,200 kiloliters of wheat, and 2,200 kiloliters of barley. They paid it that year and for each of the next two years.
\p
\v 6 Jotham faithfully obeyed Yahweh his God, and as a result, he became a powerful king.
\p
\v 7 The book in which the kings of Israel and Judah recorded what happened during their reigns describes further things that Jotham did, including the battles that he fought and how he lived.
\v 8 Jotham had been 25 years old when he became king, and he ruled from Jerusalem for 16 years.
\v 9 Then he died, and the Judeans buried him in the part of Jerusalem that people called the City of David. His son Ahaz became the next king.
\c 28
\p
\v 1 Ahaz was 20 years old when he became the king of Judah. He ruled from Jerusalem for 16 years. He did not do things that Yahweh considered to be right, as his ancestor David had done.
\v 2 Instead, he lived in the sinful way that the kings of Israel lived. He even had his workers make metal images of the god Baal {that he worshiped as idols}.
\v 3 He burned incense {to false gods} in the Valley of Ben Hinnom. He even burned his own sons as sacrifices to false gods. He did the same disgusting things as the people whom Yahweh forced to leave the land of Canaan so that the Israelites could live there.
\v 4 He offered sacrifices and burned incense {to idols} at shrines, on hilltops, and under many big trees.
\p
\v 5 As a result, Yahweh, the God he should have worshiped, allowed the army of the king of Aram to defeat his army. The Aramean army won a great victory over him and captured many Judean soldiers and took them as prisoners to Damascus. Yahweh also allowed the army of the king of Israel to defeat the army of Judah. The Israelite soldiers killed very many of the Judean soldiers.
\v 6 In one day, the {Israelite} army of Pekah son of Remaliah killed 120,000 capable Judean soldiers. That happened because the people of Judah had abandoned Yahweh, the God whom their ancestors had worshiped.
\v 7 Zichri, a great soldier from the tribe of Ephraim, killed King Ahaz’s son Maaseiah. He also killed Azrikam, who was in charge of the palace, and Elkanah, the king’s most important official.
\v 8 The Israelite soldiers captured 200,000 Judeans, who were their kinsmen. This included many of the wives, sons, and daughters of the Judean soldiers. The Israelites also took many valuable possessions from them and brought them back to Samaria.
\p
\v 9 But a prophet of Yahweh whose name was Oded lived in Samaria. He went out of the city to meet the soldiers as they were returning. He told them, “Pay attention! Yahweh, the God whom your ancestors worshiped, was angry with the people of Judah. That is why he allowed you to defeat them. But you killed them in a very cruel way. God in heaven saw what you did.
\v 10 And now you want {to do a further bad thing} by making men and women from Jerusalem and other places in Judah your slaves. But consider that you have already done many things to offend Yahweh, the God whom you should worship.
\v 11 Now listen to me! Yahweh is extremely angry with you for taking your own kinsmen as prisoners. So you must allow them to return home.”
\p
\v 12 Then some of the leaders of the tribe of Ephraim rebuked the soldiers who were returning from the battle. Those leaders were Azariah son of Johanan, Berekiah son of Meshillemoth, Jehizkiah son of Shallum, and Amasa son of Hadlai.
\v 13 They told the soldiers, “We are guilty of committing many sins against Yahweh. Yahweh is already very angry with us Israelites. So what you are doing will make Yahweh consider us even more guilty for the sins we have committed. So you must not bring those prisoners here!”
\p
\v 14 So while their leaders and the people of Samaria watched, the soldiers released the prisoners. They also returned to them the valuable possessions that they had taken.
\v 15 The leaders assigned some men to care for the prisoners. These men took some of the clothes that the soldiers had taken from the people of Judah and gave those clothes to any of the people who lacked clothing. They also gave sandals to prisoners who did not have any. They made sure that they all had enough to eat and drink, and they gave them olive oil to rub on their wounds. They provided donkeys for anyone to ride who was too weak to walk. Then they brought them all to Jericho, which was on the border of Judah. (People also call Jericho the City of Palms.) Then the Israelites who had been helping the prisoners returned to Samaria.
\p
\v 16 About that time, King Ahaz sent a message to the king of Assyria asking him to help him.
\v 17 The Edomite army had also come and attacked Judah and taken away many people as prisoners.
\v 18 Philistine soldiers had also raided cities in the foothills and in the southern Judean wilderness. They had captured the cities of Beth Shemesh, Aijalon, and Gederoth. They had also captured Soko, Timnah, and Gimzo and the surrounding villages. Philistines settled in those places.
\v 19 King Ahaz had encouraged the people of Judah to do wicked things, and he had disobeyed Yahweh greatly. So those things happened because Yahweh was punishing the Judeans for what Ahaz had led them to do.
\v 20 Tilgath Pilneser, the king of Assyria, led his army to that area. But instead of helping Ahaz, he caused trouble for him.
\v 21 Ahaz took some valuable things from Yahweh’s temple and from his royal palace and from the leaders of Judah. He sent them to the Assyrian king. But this only made Ahaz poorer
\p
\v 22 While he was experiencing those troubles, King Ahaz disobeyed Yahweh even more.
\v 23 The army of the Aramean kingdom of Damascus had defeated the army of Ahaz. Ahaz thought, “The gods that the Aramean kings worship are helping them. So I will worship those gods, and then they will help me too.” So Ahaz offered sacrifices to those gods. But worshiping those gods caused great trouble for Ahaz and his whole kingdom.
\p
\v 24 Ahaz collected the items that the priests used in God’s temple and cut them into pieces. He locked the doors of Yahweh’s temple and set up his own altars {for worshiping idols} at street corners throughout Jerusalem.
\v 25 He had his workers build hilltop shrines in cities throughout Judah. People offered sacrifices to other gods at those shrines. By doing this, Ahaz made Yahweh, the God whom his ancestors had worshiped, very angry.
\p
\v 26 The book in which the kings of Judah and Israel recorded what happened during their reigns describes further things that Ahaz did and how he lived throughout his reign.
\v 27 When Ahaz died, the Judeans buried him within the city of Jerusalem. But they did not bury him in the royal tombs. His son Hezekiah became the next king.
\c 29
\p
\v 1 Hezekiah was 25 years old when he became the king of Judah. He ruled from Jerusalem for 29 years. His mother’s name was Abijah. She was the daughter of Zechariah.
\v 2 Hezekiah did things that Yahweh considered to be right, just as his ancestor King David had done.
\p
\v 3 During the first year that Hezekiah was ruling Judah, in the first month of the year, he unlocked the doors of the temple. He had his workers repair them.
\v 4 Then he gathered the priests and Levites in the courtyard on the east side of the temple.
\v 5 He told them, “Listen to me, all you descendants of Levi! I want you to remove from the temple area everything that people have been using to worship idols. To do that, first wash yourselves to become ritually clean. Then you will be able to make Yahweh’s temple a place that is once again set apart for him, the God whom your ancestors worshiped.
\v 6 You must cleanse the temple because our ancestors disobeyed God. They did many things that Yahweh, our God, considered to be evil. They worshiped other gods instead of him. {When they offered sacrifices,} they no longer faced the Most Holy Place where Yahweh is present in a special way. Instead, they turned their backs to that place.
\v 7 They also locked the doors of the temple, and they no longer kept the lamps burning all night. They did not burn any incense to honor Yahweh, the God of us Israelites. They did not offer sacrifices to him {each morning and evening} that the priests burned completely on the altar in temple courtyard.
\v 8 This made Yahweh very angry with the people who live in Jerusalem and other places in Judah. This has made the people of other nations very frightened that they will become like us. They hiss at us {in contempt}. You have experienced this personally.
\v 9 That is why our enemies were able to kill our ancestors in battle. That is why they were able to capture our sons and daughters and wives and take them away to other countries.
\v 10 But now I intend to make a covenant with Yahweh, the God of us Israelites, so that he will no longer be angry with us.
\v 11 You who are like my sons, Yahweh has chosen you to stand in his presence as his servants. He has chosen you to serve him by offering sacrifices. So fulfill that responsibility once again right away.”
\p
\v 12 Several leaders of the Levites responded to what King Hezekiah had said.
\q1 They included Mahath son of Amasai and Joel son of Azariah from the descendants of Kohath.
\q1 From the descendants of Merari, there were Kish son of Abdi and Azariah son of Jehallelel.
\q1 From the descendants of Gershon, there were Joah son of Zimmah and Eden son of Joah.
\q1 \v 13 From the descendants of Elizaphan, there were Shimri and Jeiel.
\q1 From the descendants of Asaph, there were Zechariah and Mattaniah.
\q1 \v 14 From the descendants of Heman, there were Jehiel and Shimei.
\q1 From the descendants of Jeduthun, there were Shemaiah and Uzziel.
\p
\v 15 Those men gathered their fellow Levites, and they all washed themselves to become ritually clean. Then they went to the temple area to remove everything that people had been using to worship idols. They worked to restore the arrangements for worship that Yahweh had described in the law of Moses. That is what the king had commanded them to do.
\v 16 The priests went into the temple building to make it a holy place again. They brought out to the temple courtyard everything that they found inside the temple that people had been using to worship idols. Then the Levites took those things out of the city down to the Kidron Valley {and burned them there}.
\v 17 The priests and Levites began this work on the first day of the first month of the year. It took them eight days to clear out the temple courtyard. It took them another eight days to clear out the temple building. But by the sixteenth day of that month, they had finished making Yahweh’s temple once again a special place for him.
\p
\v 18 Representatives of the priests and Levites went to the royal palace to speak with King Hezekiah.. They told him, “We have now removed everything idolatrous from everywhere in the temple area. This includes the altar on which the priests completely burn sacrifices and all the tools that the priests use to do that. It also includes the table on which the priests display fresh bread before Yahweh each week and all the tools that the priests use to do that.
\v 19 When Ahaz was our king, he disobeyed God, and he discarded all the items that the priests used in the temple area. But we have {found and} repaired those items, and we have dedicated them for the priests to use again. We have put them back in front of Yahweh’s altar.”
\p
\v 20 Early the next morning, King Hezekiah gathered the city officials together. They all went to the courtyard of the temple.
\v 21 They brought with them seven bulls, seven rams, and seven male lambs. They also brought seven male goats. Those goats would be an offering to ask Yahweh to forgive the sins that the king and the people of Judah had committed. This offering was also to ask Yahweh to accept the temple once again as a place where people would worship him. The king commanded the {legitimate} priests, who were descendants of Aaron, to offer those animals as sacrifices to Yahweh on the altar.
\v 22 So first the city officials slaughtered the bulls, and the priests took the blood from them and sprinkled it on the altar. Then the officials slaughtered the rams, and the priests sprinkled their blood on the altar. Then the officials slaughtered the lambs, and the priests sprinkled their blood on the altar.
\v 23 The goats were to be an offering to ask Yahweh to forgive the sins of the Judeans. Someone led the goats in front of the king and the people who had gathered in the temple courtyard. The king and the people laid their hands on those goats.
\v 24 Then the priests slaughtered the goats and sprinkled their blood on the altar. They did that as an offering to ask Yahweh to forgive the sins that all the Israelites had committed. They did that because the king had told them to burn the animals completely on the altar and make this sin offering on behalf of all the people of Israel.
\p
\v 25 The king had told the Levites to stand in the temple courtyard with cymbals, lyres, and harps. That was what King David and his prophets Gad and Nathan had commanded. They gave this commandment by Yahweh’s authority, in obedience to the messages that he had given to his prophets.
\v 26 So some Levites were standing in the temple courtyard with the musical instruments that King David had provided. The priests had trumpets.
\p
\v 27 Then Hezekiah told the priests to burn the animals completely on the altar. As soon as they started to do that, those Levites began to play the instruments that David had provided when he was the king of Israel. Some other Levites started to sing to praise Yahweh, and the priests played their trumpets, following the tune that those Levites were playing on their instruments.
\v 28 While the Levites sang and the priests played their trumpets, people throughout the crowd kept bowing down to worship Yahweh. They continued to do that until the priests had finished burning all the animals completely on the altar.
\p
\v 29 When they finished burning all the animals, the king and all the city officials knelt down and worshiped Yahweh.
\v 30 Then King Hezekiah and the city officials told the Levites to praise Yahweh by singing songs that David and the prophet Asaph had composed. So the Levites sang those songs, and they and the people became joyful, and the people bowed down and worshiped Yahweh.
\p
\v 31 Then Hezekiah said to everyone who was present, “You have now dedicated yourselves to worship only Yahweh. So bring forward to the temple courtyard animals to provide meat for feasts to thank Yahweh for what he has done for you.” So the people brought forward animals to sacrifice for meals to thank Yahweh. Some who wished also brought animals for the priests to burn completely on the altar.
\p
\v 32 Altogether the people brought 70 bulls, 100 rams, and 200 male lambs for the priests to burn completely on the altar as sacrifices to Yahweh.
\v 33 The people brought 600 bulls and 3,000 sheep to provide meat for feasts to thank Yahweh.
\v 34 It was the duty of the priests to remove the skins from the animals that they burned completely on the altar. However, the Levites had been more eager to make themselves ritually clean than the priests had been. As a result, there were not enough priests available to skin all the animals that the people offered. So the Levites, their kinsmen, helped them complete this work. They continued to help them until enough priests had made themselves ritually clean.
\p
\v 35 The Levites also helped the priests skin the animals because there were so many of them to burn completely on the altar. The priests also had to prepare offerings of wine to accompany the sacrifice of each of those animals. They also had to burn on the altar the fat from the animals that provided the meat for the feasts.
\p After this, the priests once again offered sacrifices regularly to Yahweh at his temple.
\v 36 Because they had been able to restore the worship at the temple so quickly, Hezekiah and the people of Judah recognized that God had enabled them to do this, and this made them very happy.
\c 30
\p
\v 1-3 King Hezekiah wanted people throughout the kingdoms of Israel and Judah to come and celebrate the Passover festival at the temple in Jerusalem to honor Yahweh their God. He would send messengers to invite them, and he would write a special letter to the people of the northern kingdom of Israel. However, not enough of the priests had yet made themselves ritually clean, and it would take time for people to travel to Jerusalem. So they could not celebrate the festival during the first month of the year {as the law specified}. Instead, the king and his officials and the people of Jerusalem decided to celebrate Passover the following month.
\v 4 The king and the people agreed that this was a good plan.
\v 5 So they resolved to send messengers throughout the land of Israel, from Beersheba {in the far south} to Dan {in the far north}. Those messengers invited people to come to Jerusalem to celebrate the Passover to honor Yahweh their God. They sent the messengers because previously people from throughout Israel had not come to celebrate Passover, even though Moses wrote in the law that they should.
\p
\v 6 Obeying the king’s command, messengers went throughout Israel and Judah. They brought letters that the king and his officials had written. The letters said, “Yahweh is the God whom {your ancestors} Abraham, Isaac, and Israel worshiped. The kings of Assyria have taken away many of your people, but the rest of you are still living in your land. If you Israelites become loyal to Yahweh again, he will protect you.
\v 7 Do not be like your ancestors and your kinsmen, who worshiped gods other than Yahweh, the God whom their ancestors had worshiped. You know that as a result, Yahweh punished them so severely that other people are now afraid of becoming like them.
\v 8 So do not be stubborn as your ancestors were. Pledge your loyalty to Yahweh again. Come to his temple, which he has set apart for his honor forever, to worship Yahweh your God. If you do that, he will no longer be angry with you.
\v 9 Indeed, if you are loyal to Yahweh again, the enemies who took away your kinsmen and your children will act kindly toward them. They will allow them to return to this land. This will happen because Yahweh your God is kind and merciful. If you become loyal to him again, he will treat you favorably.”
\p
\v 10 The messengers went to cities throughout the northern kingdom of Israel as far north as the territory of the tribe of Zebulun. However, most of the people they spoke to laughed at them and ridiculed them.
\v 11 However, some people from the tribes of Asher, Manasseh, and Zebulun were sorry for their sins, and they came to Jerusalem.
\v 12 And God influenced the people of Judah to make them willing together to obey the command of the king and his officials and do what Yahweh’s law required.
\p
\v 13 As a result, many people gathered in Jerusalem in the second month of the year to celebrate the Festival of Unleavened Bread. They were a very large crowd of people.
\v 14 First, they removed the altars that were in Jerusalem {for offering sacrifices} to false gods. They also removed all the altars for burning incense to false gods. They burned those altars in the Kidron Valley.
\p
\v 15 Then they killed the Passover lambs, on the fourteenth day of the second month. The priests and Levites who had not yet washed themselves to become ritually clean felt shame. They did wash themselves, and they brought animals to the temple {as sacrifices for their sins} that the priests burned completely on the altar.
\p
\v 16 Moses, that special servant of God, had written in the law where the priests should stand. The priests stood in those places. The Levites handed them bowls containing the blood {from lambs they had killed}, and the priests sprinkled that blood on the altar.
\v 17 The Levites had killed those lambs because many people in the crowd had not become ritually clean, so they could not kill the lambs themselves as sacrifices to Yahweh {as the law directed}. As a result, the Levites were responsible for killing the Passover lambs for them.
\v 18 In fact, many of the people had not become ritually clean. Many of them had come from the tribes of Ephraim, Manasseh, Issachar, and Zebulun. But they ate the Passover meal anyway, even though Moses had written in the law that people had to be clean to eat it. But Hezekiah prayed for those people. He prayed, “Yahweh, you always do what is right. I ask you to forgive
\v 19 everyone who sincerely wants to honor you, Yahweh, the God whom his ancestors worshiped. Please do that even if they have not purified themselves as they should in order to worship at your temple.”
\v 20 Yahweh agreed to do what Hezekiah asked, and he did not punish the people.
\p
\v 21 The Israelites who had come to Jerusalem celebrated the Festival of Unleavened Bread very happily for seven days. The priests and Levites sang to praise Yahweh each day and played loud musical instruments that belonged to Yahweh’s temple.
\p
\v 22 Hezekiah thanked all those Levites for devoting themselves to Yahweh and leading the people in worship so skillfully. For those seven days, the people celebrated the Passover festival. They sacrificed fellowship offerings and thanked Yahweh, the God whom their ancestors had worshiped.
\p
\v 23 Then the people who had gathered in Jerusalem agreed to celebrate for seven more days. So they celebrated joyfully for seven more days.
\v 24 So that they could do that, King Hezekiah provided 1,000 bulls and 7,000 sheep for the people to eat during the festival. The leaders also provided 1,000 bulls and 10,000 sheep for the people to eat. Many priests made themselves ritually clean {so that they could serve at this festival}.
\v 25 The people who had gathered from other places in Judah were very happy. So were the priests and Levites. So were the people who had come to Jerusalem from the northern kingdom of Israel. So were the foreigners who lived in that kingdom or in Judah who had come for the festival.
\v 26 There had not been a celebration like this one in Jerusalem since the time when David’s son Solomon was the king of Israel. So the people at the festival were very happy.
\v 27 {At the end of the festival,} the {legitimate} priests, who were descendants of Levi, stood up and asked God to make good things happen for the people. In heaven, the holy place where he lives, God heard their prayer, and he did what they asked.
\c 31
\p
\v 1 After the festival ended, the Israelites who had come to Jerusalem went out to the other cities in Judah. They smashed the stone pillars that people had set up to honor false gods. They cut down the wooden images that represented the goddess Asherah. They destroyed the altars and hilltop shrines for worshiping foreign gods. They did that throughout the territory of the tribe of Judah, and also throughout the territories of Benjamin, Ephraim, and Manasseh, until none remained. Then all of those Israelites each returned to their own homes in the cities and towns where they lived.
\p
\v 2 Hezekiah organized the priests and Levites back into groups. He had each group do the work that King David had originally assigned to it. The priests would once again burn sacrifices completely on the altar and sacrifice fellowship offerings. The Levites would once again help them and also sing and play instruments to thank and praise Yahweh in the temple courtyard.
\v 3 King Hezekiah contributed sheep from his own flocks for the sacrifices that the priests would burn completely on the altar. The priests offered those sacrifices in the morning and in the evening each day. They also offered them on Sabbath days, on New Moon festivals, and at each of the feasts that the Israelites celebrated each year. Moses had written in Yahweh’s law that they should do that.
\v 4 Hezekiah commanded the people who lived in Jerusalem to bring the priests and Levites the part of their crops that belonged to them. That would allow them to do the work that Yahweh’s law had assigned to them.
\v 5 As soon as the Israelites heard this command, they generously contributed the first grain that they harvested and the first wine and olive oil that they produced. They also contributed honey as they acquired it. They later contributed a tenth of all the crops that had grown in their fields. When they brought this to the temple, it was a very large amount.
\v 6 The people of the tribe of Judah and other Israelite tribes who were living in various cities in Judah also brought a tenth of the animals in their herds and flocks. They also contributed animals that they had set apart for Yahweh their God as freewill offerings or to pay vows. The people gave so much that they made huge piles.
\v 7 They began to bring their contributions in the third month of the year{, when they celebrated the start of the harvest}. They kept contributing until the seventh month{, when they celebrated the end of the harvest}.
\v 8 When Hezekiah and his officials came and saw the huge piles, they praised Yahweh and asked him to make good things happen for the people who had given these gifts.
\p
\v 9 Then Hezekiah asked the priests and Levites how they had so much food that piles of it remained.
\v 10 Azariah the high priest, a descendant of Zadok, replied, “Since the time that the people started to bring their offerings to the temple, we have been able to eat all that we need and still have plenty remaining. This has happened because Yahweh has enabled his people to grow many crops. As a result, this great amount of food remains.
\p
\v 11 So Hezekiah told them to arrange to keep these supplies in storerooms at the temple. So they arranged to do that.
\v 12 Then they put in those storerooms the people’s contributions of the first grain they had harvested, the tenth of their crops, and the animals that people had set apart to give to Yahweh. They did this honestly. A Levite whose name was Conaniah was in charge of these supplies. His younger brother Shimei was his assistant.
\v 13 {The high priest} Azariah was in charge of everything at the temple. He and King Hezekiah appointed several men to help Conaniah and his brother Shimei manage the supplies. Those men were Jehiel, Azaziah, Nahath, Asahel, Jerimoth, Jozabad, Eliel, Ismakiah, Mahath and Benaiah.
\p
\v 14 Kore son of Imnah, a Levite who guarded the east gate of the temple courtyard, was in charge of the contributions that people made voluntarily to God. He distributed to the priests their portion of the thanksgiving offerings and the parts of sacrifices that only they could eat.
\v 15 Several Levites helped him distribute the contributions to priests and Levites who were in other cities and towns. They were Eden, Miniamin, Jeshua, Shemaiah, Amariah and Shecaniah. They distributed them honestly to those groups of priests and Levites. They did not give greater amounts to more important people.
\p
\v 16 However, they did not distribute food for any males in priestly families when they came to Jerusalem to serve in the temple. Hezekiah had organized the priests back into groups based on their ancestry to perform certain duties as their daily work. Their sons who were at least three years old accompanied them.
\v 17 (Hezekiah had organized the priests by ancestry based on their clans.) Those Levites also did not distribute food to Levites who were at least 20 years old who were performing the duties of their groups.
\v 18 But they did distribute food to the families of the priests and Levites whom Hezekiah had organized by ancestry. This included their wives, their younger sons, and their daughters. They distributed food to the priests’ and Levites’ entire families. They did that because the priests and Levites were faithfully devoting themselves to the work at the temple.
\p
\v 19 Some {legitimate} priests who were descendants of Aaron lived in in the pasturelands around the cities of Judah. Hezekiah appointed specific men in each city to distribute food to all the men who were priests. They also distributed food to all the men who could show that they were Levites by ancestry.
\p
\v 20 Hezekiah did this kind of thing throughout Judah. He did things that Yahweh his God considered to be good, right, and honest.
\v 21 Hezekiah undertook many things in order to honor God. He restored the worship at the temple, and he obeyed the commandments in the law. He did all of these things wholeheartedly. As a result, he was successful.
\c 32
\p
\v 1 After King Hezekiah had done these things so loyally to Yahweh, King Sennacherib of Assyria led his army to invade Judah. He besieged the cities that had walls around them. He thought that he would capture those cities.
\v 2 Hezekiah learned that Sennacherib had invaded Judah, and he recognized that he intended to attack Jerusalem.
\v 3-4 So he discussed with his officers and soldiers what they should do. They agreed that plenty of water should not be available to the king of Assyria and his army when they got to Jerusalem. So they decided to block the springs of water that were outside the city. Many people came to help, and they blocked all the springs of water. As a result, the Gihon stream, which flowed through that area, dried up.
\v 5 Then Hezekiah strengthened Jerusalem’s defenses. He had his workers repair the sections of the city wall that had damage. He also had them repair the outer wall and the defensive towers on the main walls. He had them strengthen the Millo on the east side of the City of David. He also had them make a large number of weapons and shields.
\p
\v 6 Hezekiah appointed army commanders to direct groups of people in Jerusalem {to defend the city}. He asked the people to gather in the square inside one of the city gates so that he could speak to them. He encouraged them by saying,
\v 7 “Be very brave. Do not be at all afraid of the king of Assyria, even though he has a huge army with him. Recognize that Yahweh is helping us, and his power is much greater than their power.
\v 8 They have only human strength, but we have Yahweh our God helping us and fighting battles for us.” The people became more confident because of what Hezekiah, the king of Judah, told them.
\p
\v 9 Then Sennacherib sent some of his officers to Jerusalem with a message for King Hezekiah and the people of Judah who lived in Jerusalem or who had gone there for protection. Sennacherib and his army were besieging the city of Lachish at the time. But his messengers said,
\v 10 “Sennacherib, the king of Assyria, says that you should not stay in Jerusalem and try to defend yourselves against his army. You should not believe that you can do that successfully.
\v 11 Hezekiah is telling you that Yahweh your God will rescue you from my power. But what he says is not true. If you believe him, you will die from having no food to eat and no water to drink.
\v 12 {Yahweh will not help} Hezekiah{, because he} has commanded his servants to destroy the hilltop shrines and altars where you could have worshiped Yahweh. He has told you people who live in Jerusalem and other places in Judah that you must worship and offer sacrifices only at the altar in the temple courtyard.
\v 13-14 You must certainly know what I and the kings who ruled Assyria before me have done to the people of other countries. Certainly none of the gods that they worshiped were able to rescue them from our power. The kings who ruled before me completely destroyed many nations, and you cannot name a single god that those nations worshiped that was able to rescue them from their power. So you should not believe that your God will be able to rescue you from my power.
\v 15 So do not believe what Hezekiah is telling you. If you do, he will deceive you into doing the wrong thing. Recognize that no god of any nation or kingdom has been able to rescue his people from my power or from the power of the kings who ruled Assyria before me. So your God will certainly not be able to rescue you from my power!”
\p
\v 16 Sennacherib’s officers said more bad things about Yahweh, the true God, and about Hezekiah, the king who served him loyally.
\v 17 King Sennacherib also wrote letters to insult Yahweh, the God of the Israelites. He said bad things about him. He wrote, “No god of any nation anywhere else has rescued his people from my power. In the same way, the God of Hezekiah will not rescue his people from my power.”
\v 18 The officers {who had come to Jerusalem} shouted in the Hebrew language to people who were standing on the city wall. They were trying to make them very afraid {so they would not fight well and} the army of Assyria would capture the city.
\v 19 They talked about the God whose temple was in Jerusalem the way they talked about the gods that people worshiped in other countries. But those gods were just idols that people had made.
\p
\v 20 King Hezekiah and the prophet Isaiah son of Amoz prayed to God desperately about this.
\v 21 Then Yahweh sent an angel who killed all the soldiers, officers, and commanders in the place where the army of the king of Assyria had set up its tents. This disgraced the king of Assyria, and he retreated to his own country. One day when he went into the temple of his god, some of his own sons killed him there with their swords.
\p
\v 22 That is how Yahweh rescued Hezekiah and the people of Jerusalem from the power of Sennacherib, the king of Assyria. and from the power of all their other enemies. Yahweh kept the countries around them from attacking them.
\v 23 After this, the people of other nations greatly respected Hezekiah. Many people brought offerings for Yahweh to Jerusalem, and they also brought valuable gifts for King Hezekiah.
\p
\v 24 Around this time, Hezekiah became so sick that he was in danger of dying. But he prayed to Yahweh, and Yahweh told him {through the prophet Isaiah} that he would live. Yahweh gave Hezekiah a special sign to confirm this.
\v 25 But Hezekiah had become very proud, so he did not make an offering at the temple to thank Yahweh for helping him. This made Yahweh angry with him and with the people who lived in Jerusalem and other places in Judah.
\v 26 But then Hezekiah recognized it was wrong to be so proud, and so did the people who lived in Jerusalem. So Yahweh did not punish them during the remaining years that Hezekiah was their king.
\p
\v 27 Hezekiah became very rich, and people honored him greatly. He had his workers make storerooms for his silver, gold, precious stones, spices, shields, and other valuable things.
\v 28 He also had them build buildings to store the grain, wine, and olive oil that his lands produced. He also had them make stalls for the various kinds of cattle he owned and pens for his flocks of sheep.
\v 29 God enabled him to acquire so much livestock that he had his workers build enclosures where he could safely keep his large flocks of sheep and herds of cattle.
\p
\v 30 Hezekiah was the one who had his workers block the higher place where the water was flowing out of the Gihon spring and build a tunnel to bring the water {into the city} on the west side of the City of David. Hezekiah successfully completed all of his projects.
\v 31 But as a result, God wanted to know what kind of person Hezekiah had become. To find out, God did not influence him to respond in a certain way when the rulers of Babylon sent messengers to him. They sent them to ask about the miracle that God had performed in the land of Judah.
\p
\v 32 The book in which the kings of Judah and Israel recorded what happened during their reigns describes further things that Hezekiah did and how he remained faithful to Yahweh. The prophet Isaiah son of Amoz wrote this account of his reign.
\v 33 When Hezekiah died, the Judeans buried him near the tombs where they had buried the previous kings who were descendants of David. People who lived in Jerusalem and throughout the kingdom of Judah honored him when he died. His son Manasseh became the next king.
\c 33
\p
\v 1 Manasseh was 12 years old when he began to rule. He ruled Judah for 55 years in Jerusalem.
\v 2 He did many things that Yahweh considered to be evil. He imitated the disgusting practices of the nations that Yahweh had forced to leave the land of Canaan so that the Israelites could settle there.
\v 3 His father Hezekiah had destroyed the shrines where the Judeans had worshiped on the tops of hills. But Manasseh commanded his workers to rebuild those shrines. He had his workers build altars for Baal. He also had them set up poles for the worship of the goddess Asherah. Manasseh also bowed down to the sun, moon, and stars to worship them.
\v 4 He directed his workers to build altars {for worshiping foreign gods} and to put them inside Yahweh’s temple, even though Yahweh had said, “Jerusalem will always be the place where people worship me.”
\v 5 He also had his workers make altars for the sun, moon, and stars and put them in the courtyards for the priests and the people in the temple area.
\v 6 He even burned his own sons as sacrifices to false gods. He did that in the Valley of Ben Hinnom. He also performed rituals and looked for omens to try to predict the future, and he practiced sorcery. He allowed people to dig pits so they could use them try to contact people who had died. People also used those pits to try to talk to spirits that they thought could tell them things that people otherwise could not know. He did very many things that Yahweh considered to be evil, and that made Yahweh very angry.
\p
\v 7 He placed inside the temple building the image he had made that represented a false god. That was the place about which God had said to David and his son Solomon, “I will make Jerusalem, and especially this temple, places where people will always worship me. I chose Jerusalem for that purpose from among all of the cities in the land of Israel.
\v 8 And if the Israelite people carefully obey all my commands and all the different kinds of laws that I gave to them through Moses, I will not make them wander again. They will be able to stay in this land that I gave to their ancestors.”
\v 9 But Manasseh persuaded the people who lived in Jerusalem and other places in Judah to commit sins that were even worse than the sins that the people of the nations committed whom Yahweh destroyed so that the Israelites could settle in the land of Canaan.
\p
\v 10 Yahweh sent prophets to warn Manasseh and the people of Judah, but they ignored them.
\v 11 So Yahweh allowed the king of Assyria’s army commanders to lead their soldiers to attack Jerusalem. They captured Manasseh, put a hook in his nose and bronze chains on his feet, and took him to Babylon.
\v 12 But there, while he was suffering, he humbled himself greatly in relationship to {Yahweh,} the God whom his ancestors had worshiped. He became loyal to Yahweh as his God and pleaded with him to help him.
\v 13 When he prayed to Yahweh, Yahweh {recognized that he was sincere and} determined to help him. Yahweh did what he asked and allowed him to return to Jerusalem and rule his kingdom again. Then Manasseh realized that Yahweh is the only true God.
\p
\v 14 After Manasseh returned, he had his workers strengthen the outer wall around Jerusalem and make it much higher. The section they strengthened extended from the west side of the Gihon spring in the valley to the Fish Gate and around the {hill of} Ophel. Manasseh also appointed army commanders for all the cities in Judah that had walls around them.
\v 15 Manasseh also had his workers remove from the temple the idols that represented foreign gods and the idol that he had put there. He also had them remove all the altars that he had previously told them to build on Mount Zion, where Yahweh’s temple was, and in other places in Jerusalem. He had them throw the idols and the materials from the altars out of the city.
\v 16 Then he had his workers repair Yahweh’s altar {in the temple courtyard}. He offered fellowship sacrifices on it and a special sacrifice to thank Yahweh. He told the people of Judah that they must worship only Yahweh, the God of the Israelites.
\v 17 Even so, the people continued to offer sacrifices at hilltop shrines, but they offered them only to Yahweh their God.
\p
\v 18 The book in which the kings of Israel recorded what happened during their reigns describes further things that Manasseh did. These things include the messages that prophets spoke to him on behalf of Yahweh, the God of the Israelites, and Manasseh’s prayer to God {for mercy when the Babylonians captured him}.
\v 19 The account that the prophet Hozai made of Manasseh’s reign records the serious sins that he committed. It lists the places where he built hilltop shrines for wooden images that represented the goddess Asherah and for other idols. But this account also describes how Manasseh then humbled himself and prayed to God and how God did what he asked.
\v 20 When Manasseh died, the Judeans buried him in his palace. His son Amon became the next king.
\p
\v 21 Amon was 22 years old when he became king, and he ruled in Jerusalem for two years.
\v 22 He did many things that Yahweh considered to be evil, as his father Manasseh had done {in the first part of his reign}. Manasseh’s workers had made many idols, and Amon sacrificed to them and worshiped them.
\v 23 Amon did not humble himself in relationship to Yahweh as his father had done. Instead, he was even more sinful than his father had been.
\p
\v 24 Amon’s officials made a secret plan to kill him. They assassinated him in his palace.
\v 25 But the people of Judah then executed all those who had assassinated Amon. They appointed his son Josiah to be the next king.
\c 34
\p
\v 1 Josiah was 8 years old when he became the king of Judah. He ruled from Jerusalem for 31 years.
\v 2 He did things that Yahweh considered to be right. He lived as his ancestor King David had lived. He did not live any differently.
\p
\v 3 After he had been ruling for almost eight years, while he was still a boy, he began to worship God sincerely, as his ancestor King David had done. Four years later, he began to make Jerusalem and the rest of the kingdom of Judah acceptable places to worship Yahweh. He did that by destroying the hilltop shrines where there were wooden images that represented the goddess Asherah, metal images, and other kinds of idols.
\v 4 He personally accompanied his workers as they destroyed the altars where people worshiped Baal. There were idols representing the sun above those altars, and the workers cut them down. They smashed the wooden poles that represented the goddess Asherah, the metal images, and the other kinds of idols. They ground them into powder and spread the powder over the graves of the people who had offered sacrifices to those false gods.
\v 5 They burned the bones of the priests who had offered sacrifices to those gods on the altars they had used to do that. That is how Josiah made Jerusalem and the rest of the kingdom of Judah acceptable places to worship Yahweh once again.
\v 6 Then he went with his workers to the cities in the territories of the tribes of Manasseh, Ephraim, and Simeon. They went as far north as the territory of the tribe of Naphtali. The enemies of the Israelites had attacked and ruined the cities throughout those areas.
\v 7 Josiah’s workers also destroyed the altars to foreign gods in those places. They broke apart the wooden poles that represented the goddess Asherah, and they ground the idols to powder. Throughout the land of Israel, they also cut up the idols that represented the sun. Then Josiah returned to Jerusalem.
\p
\v 8 When Josiah had been ruling for almost 18 years, he decided to do something further to make his kingdom and the temple acceptable places to worship Yahweh. He sent Shaphan son of Azaliah and Maaseiah, the governor of the city, and Joah son of Joahaz, his royal secretary, to repair Yahweh’s temple.
\p
\v 9 They went to Hilkiah the high priest and gave him the money that people had brought to the temple. It was money that the Levites who guarded the entrances to the temple courtyard had collected from people who had come there. They included people from the tribes of Manasseh and Ephraim and people who had survived when enemies had conquered the northern kingdom of Israel. They also included people who lived in Jerusalem and other places in the territories of the tribes of Judah and Benjamin.
\p
\v 10 Then Hilkiah and his fellow priests gave the money to the men they appointed to supervise the work of repairing the temple. Those supervisors paid the men who did the actual work of repairing and strengthening Yahweh’s temple.
\v 11 They also gave some of the money to the carpenters and builders to buy stones that workers had shaped and timber for supports and beams for the temple and the buildings around it. The kings of Judah had not cared well for those buildings.
\p
\v 12 The workers did their work faithfully. The men whom the priests appointed to supervise them included the Levites Jahath and Obadiah, who were descendants of Merari. They also included Zechariah and Meshullam, who were descendants of Kohath. (People respected these Levites who were skillful at playing musical instruments.)
\v 13 They also supervised the men who carried heavy loads and the foremen of all the crews that did various kinds of work. Other Levites were scribes, officials, and gatekeepers.
\p
\v 14 One day the priests went to the temple to give the supervisors money that people had brought to the temple. While he was in the temple, the high priest Hilkiah found a scroll that recorded the laws that Yahweh had given to Moses.
\v 15 So Hilkiah told Shaphan, the king’s secretary, “I have found a scroll in the temple that records the laws that God gave to Moses.” Hilkiah gave the scroll to Shaphan{, and he read it}.
\p
\v 16 Then Shaphan brought the scroll to King Josiah. He started by giving him a report about the temple repairs. He told him, “I gave the priests all your instructions, and they are obeying them.
\v 17 They have emptied the chest in which they were collecting money that people brought to the temple. They have given the money to the men who are supervising the work so that they can pay the workers who are repairing the temple.”
\v 18 Then Shaphan also told the king, “The high priest Hilkiah gave me this scroll.” And Shaphan read to the king what the scroll recorded.
\p
\v 19 When the king heard the laws that Shaphan was reading to him from the scroll, he tore his robes {to show how concerned he was that Yahweh would punish the Judeans}.
\v 20 Then King Josiah gave special instructions to Hilkiah, Ahikam son of Shaphan, Akbor son of Micaiah, Shaphan his royal secretary, and Asaiah, his special advisor. He told them,
\v 21 “Now that Hilkiah has found this scroll, I want you to go and ask Yahweh what I and the surviving Israelites and the people of Judah should do about what it says. Our ancestors did not obey Yahweh’s commandments. They should have done everything that this scroll records. So Yahweh must be very angry with us.”
\p
\v 22 So Hilkiah and the other men whom the king had sent went to consult a woman whose name was Huldah. She was a prophetess, and she lived in the Second Quarter of Jerusalem. She was the wife of Shallum, whose father was Tikvah and whose grandfather was Harhas. Shallum took care of the garments that the priests wore in the temple. Those five men told Huldah about the scroll that Hilkiah had found.
\p
\v 23 In response, she told them, “Yahweh, the God whom we Israelites worship, says that he wants you to say something to the man who sent you to me.
\v 24 Yahweh says that he is going to make something terrible happen to this city and to the people who live here. He is going to make all the bad things happen that the scroll people read to King Josiah describes.
\v 25 He is going to do that because they stopped worshiping him and burned incense to {honor} other gods. They made many idols, and that has made him very angry. He is so angry with them that he is certainly going punish them.
\v 26 But there is something further that you are to tell the king of Judah, who sent you to ask Yahweh about what the scroll said. Yahweh, the God whom we Israelites worship, says that
\v 27 he is going to treat him mercifully. Yahweh will do that because he was so sad and he acted humbly toward him when he heard what Yahweh said he was going to do to punish this city and the people who live here. {When King Josiah heard that,} he acted humbly toward Yahweh, and he tore his robes {to show his distress}, and he wept. He knew that Yahweh was watching him. That is why Yahweh declares {that he is going to treat him mercifully}.
\v 28 Yahweh wants you to tell Josiah that he is going to allow him to die {before this happens}, and the Judeans will bury him peacefully. Yahweh is still going to cause many terrible things to happen to Jerusalem and the people who live in it. But Josiah will not be alive to see that.” The officials returned to King Josiah and reported what Huldah had told them.
\v 29 Then King Josiah sent messengers to summon all the elders of Jerusalem and the other places in the kingdom of Judah.
\v 30 The king went to the temple courtyard. A great crowd of many different kinds of people from Jerusalem and other places in Judah assembled there with him. Many priests and Levites were there along with ordinary people. While they all listened, the king read to them all the laws that the scroll recorded that Yahweh had given the Israelites when he made his solemn agreement with them. Hilkiah had found that scroll in the temple.
\p
\v 31 The king was standing next to a pillar {on the temple porch}. He made a solemn agreement with Yahweh that he would live as Yahweh wanted him to live. He promised to obey all of Yahweh’s laws wholeheartedly and enthusiastically. In that way, the terms of the solemn agreement that the scroll from the temple described became the law of Israel once again.
\p
\v 32 King Josiah made the people who had gathered from Judah and Benjamin to the temple courtyard promise to obey that solemn agreement. From then on, the people who lived in Jerusalem {and in the rest of the kingdom} obeyed the solemn agreement that they had made with Yahweh, the God whom their ancestors had worshiped.
\p
\v 33 Josiah ordered his workers to remove all the disgusting idols from throughout the territory of the Israelites. He commanded everyone who lived in his kingdom to worship only Yahweh as their God. For as long as Josiah was alive, the people did not worship gods other than Yahweh, the God whom their ancestors had worshiped.
\c 35
\p
\v 1 Then Josiah arranged for the people to celebrate the Passover Festival in Jerusalem to honor Yahweh. They slaughtered the lambs for the Passover feast on the fourteenth day of the first month {of the year}.
\v 2 Josiah told the priests to resume the work that they each did at the temple. He encouraged them to do their work well.
\v 3 The Levites had special work to do for Yahweh, including teaching the Israelites. But they had been carrying the sacred chest around {on poles} on their shoulders {to keep it safe}. But Josiah told them, “You may now put the sacred chest {safely} back in the temple that Solomon son of David built when he was the king of Israel. You may now devote yourselves to serving Yahweh your God and his Israelite people.
\v 4 Get ready {to help the people celebrate Passover}. When David and his son Solomon were kings of Israel, they wrote instructions for the work that each of your clans should do.
\p
\v 5 I want each of your clans to stand {in a different part of} the temple area. I am going to have your fellow Israelites who are not priests or Levites gather in the temple area according to their own clans. Part of each of your clans should help one of those family groups.
\v 6 To provide a Passover feast for your fellow Israelites, kill the animals for the meal. Then wash yourselves {and hand their blood to the priests}. Do what Yahweh told Moses that the Israelites should do.”
\p
\v 7 Josiah provided 30,000 young sheep and goats from his own flocks. These were enough for the Passover meals for all the Israelites who came for the feast. He also provided 3,000 bulls from his own herds {as fellowship sacrifices}.
\p
\v 8 His officials also voluntarily contributed animals for the people, the priests, and the Levites. Hilkiah, Zechariah, and Jehiel were important officials at the temple. They gave 2,600 young sheep and goats to provide Passover meals for the priests. They also gave 300 bulls {for fellowship sacrifices}.
\v 9 Some of the leaders of the Levites gave 5,000 sheep and goats to provide Passover meals for the Levites. These men were Conaniah, his younger brothers Shemaiah and Nethanel, Hashabiah, Jeiel, and Jozabad. They also gave 300 bulls {for fellowship sacrifices}.
\p
\v 10 This is how they arranged for the Passover meal. The priests stood in their places. The Levites gathered in their clans {in order to help various family groups}. That is what the king had commanded them to do.
\v 11 The Levites killed the Passover lambs and goats and handed their blood {in bowls} to the priests, who sprinkled the blood on the altar. The Levites removed the skins from the animals,
\v 12 and they also removed the parts of the animals that the priests would burn completely on the altar. They gave those parts to the various family groups that they were assisting. The families then offered those parts to Yahweh in order to obey the instructions that Moses had written in the law. The priests, Levites, and people did the same thing with the oxen.
\v 13 As the law required, the Levites roasted the meat from the lambs and goats for the Passover meal over an open fire. But they cooked differently the meat from the animals that the king and officials had consecrated for fellowship offerings. They boiled that meat in pots and kettles and pans. The Levites served both kinds of meat immediately to all the people who had come for the feast.
\v 14 The priests, the descendants of Aaron, were busy for the entire day burning the parts of the animals that belonged to Yahweh, including their fat, completely on the altar. {Since they did not have the opportunity to prepare their own meals,} after the Levites had prepared meals for the people, they prepared meals for themselves and for the priests.
\p
\v 15 The singers who were descendants of Asaph stayed in the places where King David, Asaph, Heman, and the king’s prophet Jeduthun had commanded them to stand {and sing}. The Levites who guarded each of the gates of the temple courtyard remained at their positions. Their fellow Levites prepared food for them to eat, so the singers and gatekeepers did not need to leave their places.
\p
\v 16 On that day, the priests, Levites, and people did everything necessary to celebrate the Passover Festival to honor Yahweh. That is what King Josiah had commanded them to do. They also presented offerings that the priests burned completely on Yahweh’s altar.
\v 17 The Israelites who came for the feast celebrated the Passover on that day, and then they celebrated the Festival of Unleavened Bread for seven days.
\v 18 The Israelites had not celebrated a Passover Festival like this one since the time when the prophet Samuel lived. None of the kings of Israel had celebrated the Passover as Josiah did with the priests, the Levites, and the other people of Judah and Israel who came to Jerusalem to celebrate with the people who lived there.
\v 19 They celebrated this Passover Festival when Josiah had been ruling for almost 18 years.
\p
\v 20 Josiah did all these things to restore the worship of Yahweh at his temple. Some time later, King Neco of Egypt led his soldiers to fight {the Babylonians} at the city of Carchemish near the Euphrates River. Josiah led his army to fight against them.
\v 21 Neco sent some messengers to tell Josiah, “I have no quarrel with you, King of Judah. I have not come now to attack you. Instead, I am going to fight against my enemy, the king of Babylon. God has told me to hurry. So stop opposing God, who is helping me. Otherwise, God will kill you.”
\p
\v 22 But Josiah did not do what Neco told him to do, even though God was speaking through him. He did not return to Jerusalem with his army. But he disguised himself in order to fight against Neco. He led his soldiers to fight against Neco’s army at the plain of Megiddo.
\p
\v 23 As the Egyptian archers were shooting at the Judean soldiers, one of them hit King Josiah. He told his officers, “I am badly wounded, so take me away from here.”
\v 24 So they took him out of his war chariot and put him in his traveling chariot. They brought him to Jerusalem, where he died. The Judeans buried him in the royal tombs. People throughout Jerusalem and other places in Judah mourned for him.
\p
\v 25 The prophet Jeremiah composed a sad song about Josiah because he had died. Many men and women still sing sad songs that tell about Josiah. The Judean leaders gave orders to teach these songs to the people. The collection of laments records them.
\p
\v 26-27 The book in which the kings of Israel and Judah recorded what happened during their reigns describes further things that Josiah did throughout his reign. This includes how he faithfully obeyed Yahweh’s laws, which Moses wrote down.
\c 36
\p
\v 1 Then the Judeans appointed Josiah’s son Jehoahaz as the next king in Jerusalem.
\p
\v 2 Joahaz was 23 years old when he became the king, but he ruled in Jerusalem for only three months.
\v 3 Then the king of Egypt came and took him away. He also forced the people of Judah to pay him three and one third metric tons of silver and thirty-three kilograms of gold.
\v 4 The king of Egypt appointed Jehoahaz’s younger brother Eliakim to be the next king of the people who lived in Jerusalem and in other places in Judah. He changed Eliakim’s name to Jehoiakim. But Neco took Joahaz to Egypt as his prisoner.
\p
\v 5 Jehoiakim was 25 years old when he became the king of Judah, and he ruled from Jerusalem for 11 years. He did many things that Yahweh, the God of the Israelites, considered to be evil.
\v 6 Then the army of King Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon attacked Jehoiakim’s army. They captured Jehoiakim, bound him with bronze chains, and took him away to Babylon.
\v 7 Nebuchadnezzar’s soldiers also took some of the articles that the priests used in Yahweh’s temple. They brought them to Babylon, and Nebuchadnezzar put them in the temple of his god.
\p
\v 8 The book in which the kings of Israel and Judah recorded what happened during their reigns describes further things that Jehoiakim did. This includes the disgusting acts he committed and what a bad person he was. His son Jehoiachin became the next king of Judah.
\p
\v 9 Jehoiachin was 18 years old when he became the king of Judah. He ruled from Jerusalem for only three months and ten days. He did many things that Yahweh considered to be evil.
\v 10 During the spring of the next year, King Nebuchadnezzar sent soldiers to bring him to Babylon. They also took away many valuable things from Yahweh’s temple. Nebuchadnezzar appointed Jehoiachin’s uncle Zedekiah to rule instead over the people who lived in Jerusalem and in other parts of Judah.
\p
\v 11 Zedekiah was 21 years old when he became the king, and he ruled from Jerusalem for 11 years.
\v 12 He did many things that Yahweh, the God of the Israelites, considered to be evil. Yahweh gave the prophet Jeremiah a message to warn him. But when Zedekiah heard that message, he was not sorry for the wrong things he had been doing.
\v 13 He was very proud, and he was very stubborn. So he did not start obeying Yahweh, the God of the Israelites, once again. King Nebuchadnezzar had made him promise to be loyal to him. He had made him say that he hoped God would punish him if he broke that promise. Even so, Zedekiah rebelled against Nebuchadnezzar.
\v 14 Most of the leaders of the priests and the people of Judah also did many very wicked things. They did the same kind of disgusting things as the people had done whom Yahweh forced to leave the land of Canaan. Yahweh had made his temple in Jerusalem a special place where he was present. But those leaders put idols in the temple area, so that Yahweh no longer considered it an acceptable place to worship him.
\p
\v 15 Yahweh was the God whom the ancestors of the people of Judah had worshiped. He wanted to be merciful to his people, and he did not want anyone to destroy his temple. So he continually sent prophets to them with messages to warn them.
\v 16 But the people laughed at the prophets. They treated God’s messages as if they were not important. They treated the prophets very badly. Finally Yahweh became so angry with his people that he decided he would certainly destroy Judah.
\v 17 He influenced the king of Babylon to attack Judah with his army. They killed the Judean soldiers with their swords, even those who went to the temple area for safety. They did not spare anyone, regardless of their sex or age. God enabled the army of Nebuchadnezzar to conquer the entire city.
\v 18 His soldiers took to Babylon all the items of every size that the priests used in God’s temple. They also took the valuable things that were in the storerooms at the temple and the valuable things that belonged to the king and his officials.
\v 19 They burned the temple, and they broke down the wall around Jerusalem. They burned all the important buildings in Jerusalem, and they destroyed all the valuable things that they did not carry away.
\p
\v 20 There were some people whom the soldiers had not killed. They took those people away as prisoners to Babylon. Those people had to work for Nebuchadnezzar and the kings who ruled Babylon after him until the army of Persia conquered Babylon.
\v 21 Those people remained in Babylon for 70 years. During that time, no one planted or harvested crops in their land. As a result, the soil recovered, as if would have done if the people had obeyed the law of Moses and not planted crops every seventh year. Yahweh had said through Jeremiah that this would happen, and it did.
\p
\v 22 During the first year after Cyrus, king of Persia, {conquered the kingdom of Babylon,} Yahweh motivated Cyrus to write a message. Yahweh did this in order to make something happen that Jeremiah had predicted. Cyrus sent messengers throughout his empire with copies of the message, and he ordered them to proclaim it as they went. This is what they proclaimed:
\p
\v 23 “I, King Cyrus, rule the Persian Empire, and I say, ‘Yahweh, the God who is in heaven, has made me ruler over the great kingdoms of this part of the world. Now he has assigned me to {make sure that his people} build a temple for him in {the city of} Jerusalem in {the province of} Judah. Any of you who belong to his people may go {to Jerusalem in Judah to rebuild this temple for Yahweh}. And may Yahweh your God help you!’” 